\h 2 Chronicles
\toc1 The Second Book of the Chronicles
\toc2 Second Chronicles
\toc3 2Ch
\mt1 Second Chronicles
\c 1
\p
\v 1 And Solomon, the son of David, strengthened himself over his kingdom, and Yahweh his God {was} with him and made him great to above.
\v 2 And Solomon spoke to all of Israel, to the commanders of thousands and hundreds and to the judges and to every leader in all of Israel, the heads of fathers.
\v 3 And Solomon went, and all of the assembly with him, to the high place that {was} at Gibeon, for the tent of meeting of God was there, which Moses, the servant of Yahweh, had made in the wilderness.
\v 4 But David had brought up the Box of God from Kiriath Jearim to {where} David had prepared for it, for he had stretched out a tent for it in Jerusalem.
\v 5 And the altar of bronze that Bezalel, the son of Uri, the son of Hur, had made {was} there to the face of the tabernacle of Yahweh, and Solomon and the assembly sought him.
\v 6 And Solomon went up there to the altar of bronze, to the face of Yahweh, which {was} at the tent of meeting, and he brought up onto it 1,000 burnt offerings.
\p
\v 7 In that night, God appeared to Solomon, and he said to him, “Ask! What I shall give to you?”
\v 8 And Solomon said to God, “You, you did great covenant faithfulness with David my father, and you have made me reign in his place.
\v 9 Now, Yahweh God, may your word with David my father be confirmed, for you, you have made me reign over a people numerous as the dust of the earth.
\v 10 Now give wisdom and knowledge to me, that I may go out to the face of this people and that I may come in, for who shall judge this your great people?”
\v 11 And God said to Solomon, “Because this was with your heart, and you have not requested wealth, riches, or honor or the life of the ones hating you, and you have also not requested many days, but you have requested wisdom and knowledge for yourself that you might judge my people, over whom I have made you reign,
\v 12 wisdom and knowledge are given to you, and I will give to you wealth and riches and honor that were not thus to the kings who were to your face and will not be thus after you.”
\v 13 And Solomon came from the high place that {was} at Gibeon {to} Jerusalem, from the face of the tent of meeting, and he reigned over Israel.
\p
\v 14 And Solomon accumulated chariotry and horsemen. And 1,400 chariots and 12,000 horsemen were to him. And he stationed them in the cities of chariotry and with the king in Jerusalem.
\v 15 And the king made silver and gold in Jerusalem like stones, and he made cedars like the sycamores that {are} in the Shephelah for abundance.
\v 16 And the going out of the horses that {were} to Solomon {was} from Egypt and from Kue. The traders of the king took from Kue at a price.
\v 17 And they brought up and brought out from Egypt a chariot for 600 silver and a horse for 150. And thus by their hand they brought out to all of the kings of the Hittites and the kings of Aram.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 And Solomon said to build a house for the name of Yahweh and a house for his kingdom.
\v 2 And Solomon numbered 70,000, a man, a bearer, and 80,000, a man, a quarrier in the hill country, and ones overseeing over them, 3,600.
\v 3 And Solomon sent to Hiram, the king of Tyre, saying, “As that you did with David my father and sent to him cedars to build for himself a house to dwell in it,
\v 4 behold, I am building a house for the name of Yahweh my God, to consecrate to him, to burn to his face incense of spices, and for the perpetual arrangement and burnt offerings in the morning and in the evening, on the Sabbaths and on the new moons and at the annual festivals of Yahweh our God. Forever this {is} upon Israel.
\v 5 Now the house that I am building {shall be} great, for our God {is} greater than all gods.
\v 6 But who can retain strength to build a house for him, since the heavens and the heavens of the heavens do not contain him? And who {am} I, that I should build a house for him, except to sacrifice to his face?
\v 7 And now, send to me a wise man to work in gold and in silver and in bronze and in iron and in purple and crimson and violet, one also knowing {how} to engrave engravings, with the wise who {are} with me in Judah and in Jerusalem, whom David my father prepared.
\v 8 And send to me trees of cedar, cypress, and algum from Lebanon, for I, I know that your servants are knowing {how} to cut trees of Lebanon. And behold, my servants {will be} with your servants,
\v 9 even to prepare for me trees in abundance, for the house that I am building {will be} great, and it will be wonderful.
\v 10 And behold, to the hewers, to the cutters of the trees, I will give crushed wheat to your servants, 20,000 cors, and barley, 20,000 cors, and wine 20,000 baths, and oil, 20, 000 baths.”
\p
\v 11 And Hiram, the king of Tyre, said in a writing that he sent to Solomon, “Because Yahweh loves his people, he has made you king over them.”
\v 12 And Hiram said, “Blessed {be} Yahweh, the God of Israel, who made the heavens and the earth, who has given to King David a son of wisdom, knowing insight and understanding, who will build a house for Yahweh and a house for his kingdom.
\v 13 And now, I have sent a wise man, knowing understanding, Huram Abi,
\v 14 the son of a woman from the daughters of Dan, and his father a man of Tyre, knowing {how} to work in gold and in silver, in bronze, in iron, in stones and in wood, in purple, in violet, and in linen and in crimson, and {how} to engrave any engraving and to devise any device that is given to him, with your wise {ones} and the wise {ones} of my lord David, your father.
\v 15 And now, the wheat and the barley, the oil and the wine that my lord has said, let him send to his servants.
\v 16 And we, we will cut trees from Lebanon, according to all of your need, and we will bring them to you {as} rafts upon the sea {to} Joppa, and you, you will bring them up {to} Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 17 And Solomon numbered all of the men, the foreigners who {were} in the land of Israel, after the counting that David his father had counted them. And they were found {to be} 153,600.
\v 18 And from them he made 70,000, a bearer, and 80,000. a quarrier in the hill country, and 3,600 ones overseeing to make the people work.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 And Solomon began to build the house of Yahweh in Jerusalem on Mount Moriah, where he had appeared to David his father, where David had prepared, in the place at the threshing floor of Ornan the Jebusite.
\v 2 And he began to build in the second month, on the second, in the year of four to his reign.
\v 3 And these {were} the founding of Solomon to build the house of God. The length {in} cubits, by the former measure, {was} 60 cubits, and the width {was} 20 cubits.
\v 4 And the porch that {was} on the face of the length on the face of the width of the house {was} 20 cubits, and its height {was} 120. And he overlaid it from within {with} pure gold.
\v 5 And he covered the great house with wood of cypress, and he covered it with pure gold, and he brought up palm trees and chains onto it.
\v 6 And he overlaid the house {with} precious stone for beauty, and the gold {was} gold of Parvaim.
\v 7 And he covered the house, the beams, the thresholds, and its walls and its doors {with} gold, and he engraved cherubs on the walls.
\p
\v 8 And he made the house of the Holy of Holies. Its length on the face of the width of the house {was} 20 cubits, and its width {was} 20 cubits. And he covered it {with} pure gold, to 600 kikkars.
\v 9 And the weight of the nails {was} to 50 shekels {of} gold. And he covered the upper chambers {with} gold.
\p
\v 10 And he made in the house of the Holy of Holies two cherubs, a work of carving, and he overlaid them {with} gold.
\v 11 And the wings of the cherubs, their length {was} 20 cubits. A wing of the one was to 5 cubits, reaching to the wall of the house, and the other wing {was} 5 cubits, reaching to a wing of the other cherub.
\v 12 And a wing of the other cherub {was} 5 cubits, reaching to the wall of the house, and the other wing {was} 5 cubits, joining to the wing of the other cherub.
\v 13 The wings of these cherubs were spreading out 20 cubits. And they were standing on their feet, and their faces {were} toward the house.
\v 14 And he made a curtain {of} violet and purple and crimson and linen, and he brought cherubs up onto it.
\p
\v 15 And he made two pillars to the face of the house, 35 cubits {was} the length, and the capital that was on its head {was} five cubits.
\v 16 And he made chains {as} in the inner sanctuary, and he put {them} on the head of the pillars, and he made 100 pomegranates, and he put {them} on the chains.
\v 17 And he set up the pillars on the face of the temple, one from the right and one from the left. And he called the name of the right Jakin and the name of the left Boaz.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 And he made an altar of bronze. Its length (was) 20 cubits, and its width (was) 20 cubits, and its height {was} 10 cubits.
\v 2 And he made a cast sea, ten by the cubit from its lip to its lip, circular around. And five by the cubit {was} its height, and a cord of thirty by the cubit surrounded it around.
\v 3 And a likeness of oxen {was} below to it around, around, surrounding it, ten in a cubit, encircling the sea around. Oxen {in} two rows were cast in its casting.
\v 4 It was standing on twelve oxen, three facing north and three facing west and three facing south and three facing east, and the sea {was} upon them from above. And all of their hindquarters {were} inward.
\v 5 And its thickness {was} a handbreadth, and its lip {was} like the work of the lip of a cup, the blossom of a lily. Taking hold, it contained 3,000 baths.
\v 6 And he made ten basins, and he put five on the right and five on the left, to wash in them. They would rinse the work of the burnt offering in them. And the Sea {was} for washing for the priests in it.
\p
\v 7 And he made ten lampstands of gold according to their ordinance, and he put five on the right and five on the left in the temple.
\v 8 And he made ten tables and rested {them} in the temple, five on the right and five on the left. And he made 100 bowls of gold.
\v 9 And he made the courtyard of the priests and the great court and the doors for the court, and he overlaid their doors {with} bronze.
\v 10 And he put the Sea from the shoulder of the right, eastward toward the south.
\v 11 And Huram made basins and shovels and bowls.
\p And Huram finished doing the work that he did for King Solomon for the house of God:
\v 12 the two pillars, and the bowls, the capitals on the head of the two pillars, and the two networks for covering the two bowls of the capitals that {were} on the head of the pillars,
\v 13 and the 400 pomegranates for the two networks, two rows of pomegranates for one network, to cover the two bowls of the capitals that {were} on the faces of the pillars.
\v 14 And he made stands, and he made basins on the stands,
\v 15 one sea and 12 oxen beneath it,
\v 16 and the pots and the shovels and the forks. And all of the vessels Huram Abi made for King Solomon for the house of Yahweh {were} polished bronze.
\v 17 In the plain of the Jordan the king cast them, in the thickness of the ground between Succoth and between Zaredathah.
\v 18 And Solomon made all of these vessels in great abundance, so the weight of the bronze was not sought out.
\p
\v 19 And Solomon made all of the vessels that {were} {for} the house of God: the altar of gold and the tables that on them {was} the bread of the face
\v 20 and the lampstands and their lamps for their burning according to the ordinance to the face of the inner sanctuary, purified gold,
\v 21 and the blossom and the lamps and the tongs, gold, it {was} perfections of gold,
\v 22 and the trimmers and the sprinkling bowls and the palms and the firepans, purified gold, and the entrance of the house, its inner doors to the Holy of Holies, and the doors of the house, for the temple, gold,
\c 5
\p
\v 1 And all of the work that Solomon did for the house of Yahweh was complete. And Solomon brought in the holy things of David his father. And he put the silver and the gold and all of the vessels in the storerooms of the house of God.
\p
\v 2 Then Solomon assembled the elders of Israel and all of the heads of the tribes, the leaders of the fathers of the sons of Israel, to Jerusalem to bring up the Box of the Covenant of Yahweh from the City of David, that {is}, Zion.
\v 3 And every man of Israel, they assembled to the king at the feast; it {was} the seventh month.
\v 4 And all of the elders of Israel came, and the Levites carried the Box.
\v 5 And they brought up the Box and the tent of meeting and all of the sacred vessels that {were} in the tent. The priests {and} the Levites brought them up.
\v 6 And King Solomon and all of the congregation of Israel having gathered themselves to him {were} to the face of the Box, sacrificing sheep and cattle that were not counted and were not numbered from abundance.
\v 7 And the priests brought in the Box of the Covenant of Yahweh to its place, to the inner sanctuary of the house, to the Holy of Holies, to under the wings of the cherubs.
\v 8 And the cherubs were spreading out wings over the place of the Box. And the cherubs covered over the Box and over its poles from above.
\v 9 And the poles were long, and the heads of the poles from the Box were seen at the face of the inner sanctuary, but they were not seen {from} the outside. And they are there to this day.
\v 10 Nothing {was} in the Box except the two tablets that Moses had placed at Horeb, where Yahweh cut with the sons of Israel when they came out from Egypt.
\p
\v 11 And it happened, when the priests came out from the holy place (for all of the priests who were found had consecrated themselves, without keeping to the divisions),
\v 12 and the Levites, the singers, as for all of them, Asaph, Heman, Jeduthun, and their sons and their brothers, clothed {in} linen, with cymbals and with lyres and harps, were standing east of the altar, and with them {were} 120 priests blowing trumpets,
\v 13 that it happened, the trumpeters and singers {were} like one to sound one voice to praise and thank Yahweh, and as they raised a voice with trumpets and with cymbals and with instruments of song, and when they praised Yahweh, “For {he is} good, for his covenant faithfulness {is} to eternity,” that the house, the house of Yahweh, filled {with} a cloud,
\v 14 and the priests were not able to stand to minister from the face of the cloud, for the glory of Yahweh filled the house of God.
\c 6
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon said, “Yahweh said he would dwell in a dark cloud.
\v 2 But I, I have built a house of habitation for you and a place for your dwelling {for} eternities.”
\v 3 And the king turned his face around, and he blessed all of the assembly of Israel, and all of the assembly of Israel was standing.
\p
\v 4 And he said, “Blessed {be} Yahweh, the God of Israel, who spoke by his mouth to David my father and fulfilled by his hand, saying,
\v 5 ‘From the day when I brought out my people from the land of Egypt, I did not choose a city from all of the tribes of Israel to build a house for my name to be there. And I did not choose a man to be a leader over my people Israel.
\v 6 But I chose Jerusalem for my name to be there, and I chose David to be over my people Israel.’
\v 7 And it was with the heart of David my father to build a house for the name of Yahweh, the God of Israel.
\v 8 But Yahweh said to David my father, ‘Because it was with your heart to build a house for my name, you did well that it was with your heart.
\v 9 However, you, you will not build the house, but your son coming out from your loins, he, he will build the house for my name.’
\v 10 And Yahweh has raised up his word that he spoke. And I have arisen in place of David my father, and I sit on the throne of Israel, just as Yahweh spoke. And I have built the house for the name of Yahweh, the God of Israel.
\v 11 And I have put there the Box where the Covenant of Yahweh {is} there, which he cut with the sons of Israel.”
\p
\v 12 And he stood to the face of the altar of Yahweh, in front of all of the assembly of Israel, and he spread out his palms
\v 13 (for Solomon had made a platform of bronze, and he had placed it in the midst of the court, five cubits {was} its length and five cubits {was} its width and three cubits {was} its height), and he stood on it, and he knelt on his knees in front of all of the assembly of Israel, and he spread out his palms toward the heavens
\v 14 and he said, “Yahweh, the God of Israel, no god {is} like you in the heavens or on the earth, keeping the covenant and covenant faithfulness with your servants walking to your face with all of their heart,
\v 15 which you have kept for your servant David, my father, what you spoke to him. And you spoke with your mouth, and by your hand you fulfilled, as this day.
\v 16 And now, Yahweh, the God of Israel, keep for your servant David, my father, what you spoke to him, saying, ‘A man to you will not be cut off from to my face, sitting on the throne of Israel, if only your sons will keep their way, to walk in my law, just as you have walked to my face.’
\v 17 And now, Yahweh, God of Israel, may your word be confirmed that you spoke to your servant David.
\p
\v 18 But will God truly dwell with man on the earth? Behold, the heavens and the heavens of the heavens do not contain you. How much less this house that I have built!
\v 19 Yet turn to the prayer of your servant and to his supplication, Yahweh my God, to listen to the cry and to the prayer that your servant is praying to your face,
\v 20 that your eyes may be opened to this house day and night, to the place {about} which you said you would put your name there, to listen to the prayer that your servant will pray toward this place.
\v 21 And may you listen to the supplication of your servant and your people Israel that they will pray toward this place. And you, may you hear from the place of your dwelling, from the heavens, and may you hear and forgive.
\p
\v 22 If a man sins against his neighbor, and he lifts an oath against him to adjure him, and an oath comes to the face of your altar in this house,
\v 23 then you, may you hear from the heavens and act and judge your servants, to condemn the wicked, to put his way on his head, and to acquit the righteous, to give to him according to his righteousness.
\p
\v 24 And if your people Israel are struck to the face of an enemy because they have sinned against you, and they turn back and they confess your name and they pray and they supplicate to your face in this house,
\v 25 then you, may you hear from the heavens, and may you forgive the sin of your people Israel and bring them back to the land that you gave to them and to their fathers.
\p
\v 26 When the heavens are restrained and there is no rain because they have sinned against you, and they pray toward this place and they confess your name, they turn back from their sin because you have afflicted them,
\v 27 then you, may you hear {from} the heavens, and may you forgive the sin of your servants and your people Israel, for you teach toward the good way in which they should walk. And may you give rain on your land that you have given to your people for an inheritance.
\p
\v 28 If famine is in the land, if {there} is pestilence, if {there} is blight, mildew, locust, grasshopper, if his enemy makes it narrow to him in the land of his gates, any plague, any sickness,
\v 29 any prayer, any supplication that is to any man, to any of your people Israel who know, a man, his plague and his suffering, and he spreads out his palms toward this house,
\v 30 then you, may you hear from the heavens, the place of your dwelling, and may you forgive, and may you give to a man according to all of his ways, for you know his heart. For you, you alone, you know the heart of the sons of man,
\v 31 so that they may fear you, to walk in your ways all of the days that they {are} alive on the face of the land that you gave to our fathers.
\p
\v 32 And also, concerning the foreigner, he who {is} not from your people Israel, and he comes from a distant land because of your great name and your mighty hand and your outstretched arm, and he comes and prays toward this house,
\v 33 then you, may you hear from the heavens, from the place of your dwelling, and may you act according to all that the foreigner has cried out to you, so that all of the peoples of the earth will know your name, to fear you as your people Israel {do} and to know that your name has been called over this house that I have built.
\p
\v 34 If your people goes out to battle against its enemy by the way that you send them, and they pray to you {in} the way of this city upon which you have chosen and the house that I have built for your name,
\v 35 then may you hear from the heavens their prayer and their supplication, and may you do their justice.
\p
\v 36 If they sin against you (for {there is} no man who does not sin) and you are angry with them and you give them to the face of an enemy and they take them captive {as} their captives to a land distant or near,
\v 37 and they bring back to their heart in the land where they have been taken captive there, and they turn back and supplicate to you in the land of their captivity, saying, ‘We have sinned, we have done iniquity and we have acted wickedly,’
\v 38 and they return to you with all of their heart and with all of their soul in the land of their captivity, where they took them captive, and they pray {in} the way of their land, which you gave to their fathers, and the city that you have chosen and the house that I have built for your name,
\v 39 then may you hear from the heavens, from the place of your dwelling, their prayer and their supplication, and may you do their justice, and may you forgive your people who sinned against you.
\p
\v 40 Now, my God, may your eyes please be opened and your ears attentive to the prayer of this place.
\v 41 And now arise, Yahweh God, to your resting place, you and the Box of your strength. Your priests, Yahweh God, may they be clothed {in} salvation, and may your faithful ones rejoice in goodness.
\v 42 Yahweh God, do not turn away the face of your anointed. Remember the covenant faithfulnesses of David your servant.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 And when Solomon finished praying, then fire came down from the heavens, and it devoured the burnt offering and the sacrifices, and the glory of Yahweh filled the house.
\v 2 And the priests were not able to go into the house of Yahweh, because the glory of Yahweh filled the house of Yahweh.
\v 3 And all of the sons of Israel were seeing the fire coming down and the glory of Yahweh on the house, and they bowed down, nostrils earthward on the pavement, and they prostrated themselves and gave thanks to Yahweh, “For {he is} good, for his covenant faithfulness {is} to eternity.”
\p
\v 4 And the king and all of the people were sacrificing a sacrifice to the face of Yahweh.
\v 5 And King Solomon sacrificed a sacrifice of 22,000 oxen and 120,000 sheep. And the king and all of the people dedicated the house of God.
\v 6 And the priests were standing at their duties, and the Levites with the instruments of song of Yahweh that King David had made to give thanks to Yahweh, “For his covenant faithfulness {is} to eternity,” when David praised by their hand. And the priests were trumpeting in front of them, and all of Israel was standing.
\v 7 And Solomon consecrated the middle of the courtyard that {was} to the face of the house of Yahweh, so that there he made the burnt offerings and the fat of the peace offerings, for the altar of bronze that Solomon had made was not able to contain the burnt offering and the grain offering and the fats.
\p
\v 8 And Solomon observed the feast at that time, seven days, and all of Israel with him, a very great assembly, from Lebo Hamath to the wadi of Egypt.
\v 9 And they did an assembly on the eighth day, for they did the dedication of the altar, seven days, and the feast, seven days.
\v 10 And on the day of 23 to the seventh month, he sent the people to their tents, rejoicing and good of heart over the good that Yahweh had done for David and for Solomon and for Israel his people.
\p
\v 11 And Solomon completed the house of Yahweh and the house of the king. And everything coming into the heart of Solomon to do in the house of Yahweh and in his house, he did successfully.
\v 12 And Yahweh appeared to Solomon by night, and he said to him, “I have heard your prayer, and I have chosen this place for myself for a house of sacrifice.
\v 13 If I restrain the heavens and there is no rain, or if I command to the locust to devour the land, or if I send pestilence among my people,
\v 14 and my people, over whom my name is called, humble themselves and pray and seek my face and turn from their evil ways, then I, I will hear from the heavens, and I will forgive their sin, and I will heal their land.
\v 15 Now my eyes will be opened and my ears attentive to the prayer of this place.
\v 16 And now I have chosen and I have consecrated this house for my name to be there until forever. And my eyes and my heart will be there all of the days.
\v 17 And you, if you walk to my face just as David your father walked, to do according to all that I have commanded you, and you keep my statutes and my judgments,
\v 18 then I will raise up the throne of your kingdom, just as I cut with David your father, saying, ‘A man to you will not be cut off {from} ruling in Israel.’
\p
\v 19 But if you, you turn and you forsake my statutes and my commandments that I have set to your faces and you go and serve other gods and prostrate yourselves to them,
\v 20 then I will uproot them from upon my ground that I have given to them, and this house that I have consecrated for my name, I will send away from before my face, and I will make it a proverb and a taunt among all of the peoples.
\v 21 And this house that is exalted to everyone passing by it will be appalled, and they will say, ‘Why did Yahweh do according to thus to this land and to this house?’
\v 22 And they will say, ‘Because they forsook Yahweh, the God of their fathers, who brought them out from the land of Egypt, and they held onto other gods and prostrated themselves to them and served them. Therefore he brought upon them all of this harm.’”
\c 8
\p
\v 1 And it happened at the end of 20 years, {during} which Solomon built the house of Yahweh and his house,
\v 2 that the cities that Huram gave to Solomon, Solomon built them. And he made the sons of Israel dwell there.
\p
\v 3 And Solomon went {to} Hamath Zobah, and he was stronger than it.
\v 4 And he built Tadmor in the wilderness and all of the cities of the storehouses that he built in Hamath.
\v 5 And he built Upper Beth Horon and Lower Beth Horon, cities of fortification {with} walls, doors, and a bar,
\v 6 and Baalath and all of the cities of the storehouses that were to Solomon and all of the cities of the chariotry and the cities of the horsemen and all of the desire of Solomon, whatever he desired to build in Jerusalem and in Lebanon and in all of the land of his dominion.
\p
\v 7 All of the people being left from the Hittite and the Amorite and the Perizzite and the Hivite and the Jebusite, they who {were} not from Israel,
\v 8 from their sons who were left after them in the land, whom the sons of Israel did not finish them, Solomon brought them up for the forced labor, to this day.
\v 9 But from the sons of Israel whom Solomon did not make servants for his work, instead they {were} men of war and commanders of his captains and commanders of his chariotry and his horsemen.
\v 10 And these {were} the commanders of the deputies who {were} to King Solomon, 250, ruling over the people.
\p
\v 11 And Solomon brought the daughter of Pharaoh up from the city of David to the house that he built for her, for he said, “The wife to me shall not dwell in the house of David, the king of Israel, because they {are} holy, where the Box of Yahweh has come to them.”
\p
\v 12 Then Solomon offered burnt offerings to Yahweh on the altar of Yahweh that he had built to the face of the porch,
\v 13 even by the matter of a day on a day, to offer, according to the commandment of Moses, for Sabbaths and for New Moons and for the annual festivals three times in a year, at the Festival of Unleavened Bread and at the Festival of Weeks and at the Festival of Huts.
\p
\v 14 And he established, according to the ordinance of David his father, the divisions of the priests for their service and the Levites for their duties to praise and to minister in front of the priests for the matter of a day in its day and the gatekeepers in their divisions for gate and gate, for thus {was} the commandment of David, the man of God.
\v 15 And they did not turn from the commandment of the king regarding the priests and the Levites about any matter or about the storerooms.
\p
\v 16 And all of the work of Solomon was established, from the day of the foundation of the house of Yahweh and unto its finishing. The house of Yahweh {was} complete.
\p
\v 17 Then Solomon went to Ezion Geber and to Eloth on the lip of the sea in the land of Edom.
\v 18 And Huram sent to him, by the hand of his servants, ships and servants, knowers of the sea. And they came with the servants of Solomon to Ophir. And they took from there 450 kikkars of gold, and they brought {them} to King Solomon.
\c 9
\p
\v 1 Now the Queen of Sheba heard the report of Solomon, and she came to test Solomon with difficult questions in Jerusalem with a very great entourage and camels carrying spices and gold in abundance and precious stone. And she came to Solomon and spoke with him all that was in her heart.
\v 2 And Solomon declared to her all of her matters, and no matter was hidden from Solomon that he did not declare to her.
\v 3 And the Queen of Sheba saw the wisdom of Solomon and the house that he had built
\v 4 and the food of his table and the seating of his servants and the service of his attendants and their apparel and his cupbearers and their apparel and his burnt offerings that he offered {at} the house of Yahweh. And no more breath was in her.
\v 5 And she said to the king, “The word {is} true that I heard in my land about your words and about your wisdom.
\v 6 But I did not believe their words until when I came and my eyes saw. And behold, half of the greatness of your wisdom had not been declared to me. You have added to the report that I heard.
\v 7 The happiness of your men! The happiness of these servants of yours, the ones standing to your face continually and hearing your wisdom!
\v 8 May Yahweh your God {be} blessed, who has delighted in you to put you on his throne as king for Yahweh your God. Because your God loves Israel to establish him to eternity, so he has put you over them as king, to do justice and righteousness!”
\v 9 And she gave to the king 120 kikkars of gold and spices in great abundance and precious stone. And {there} has not been like that spice which the Queen of Sheba gave to King Solomon.
\p
\v 10 (And also the servants of Hiram and the servants of Solomon who brought gold from Ophir brought wood of algum and precious stone.
\v 11 And the king made the wood of algum {into} staircases for the house of Yahweh and for the house of the king and {into} harps and lyres for the singers. And like them were not seen before in the land of Judah.)
\p
\v 12 And King Solomon gave to the Queen of Sheba all of her desire that she asked, besides what she brought to the king. And she turned and went to her land, she and her servants.
\p
\v 13 And the weight of the gold that came to Solomon in one year was 666 kikkars of gold,
\v 14 apart from {what} the men of the searchers and the merchants were bringing in. And all of the kings of the Arab and the governors of the land were bringing gold and silver to Solomon.
\v 15 And King Solomon made 200 beaten-gold large shields (he brought up 600 beaten gold onto one large shield)
\v 16 and 300 beaten-gold shields (he brought up 300 gold onto one shield). And the king put them in the House of the Forest of Lebanon.
\v 17 And the king made a great ivory throne, and he overlaid it {with} pure gold.
\v 18 And six steps {were} to the throne, and a footstool was fastened to the throne with gold. And hands {were} from this and from this to the place of the seat, and two lions were standing beside the hands.
\v 19 And 12 lions were standing there on the six steps, from this and from this. It had not been done thus for any kingdom.
\v 20 And all of the vessels of the drink of King Solomon {were} gold, and all of the vessels of the House of the Forest of Lebanon {were} beaten gold. Silver was not reckoned as anything in the days of Solomon.
\v 21 For the ships to the king were going {to} Tarshish with the servants of Huram. One in three years the ships of Tarshish would come carrying gold and silver, ivory and apes and peacocks.
\p
\v 22 And King Solomon became greater than all of the kings of the earth in wealth and wisdom.
\v 23 And all of the kings of the earth were seeking the face of Solomon to hear his wisdom, which God had put in his heart.
\v 24 And they were bringing, a man his gift, vessels of silver and vessels of gold and garments, weaponry and spices, horses and mules, a matter of year by year.
\v 25 And 4,000 stalls of horses and chariots were to Solomon, and 12,000 horsemen. And he stationed them in the cities of chariotry and with the king in Jerusalem.
\v 26 And it happened, he was ruling over all of the kings from the River and to the land of the Philistines and to the border of Egypt.
\v 27 And the king made silver in Jerusalem like stones, and he made cedars like the sycamores that {are} in the Shephelah for abundance.
\v 28 And they were bringing out horses from Egypt to Solomon and from all of the lands.
\p
\v 29 Now the rest of the matters of Solomon, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, are they not written in the words of Nathan the prophet and in the prophecy of Ahijah the Shilonite and in the visions of Iddo the seer concerning Jeroboam, the son of Nebat?
\v 30 And Solomon reigned in Jerusalem over all of Israel 40 years.
\v 31 And Solomon lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in the City of David his father. And Rehoboam his son reigned in his place.
\c 10
\p
\v 1 And Rehoboam went to Shechem, for all of Israel had come {to} Shechem to make him reign.
\v 2 And it happened when Jeroboam, the son of Nebat, heard (now he {was} in Egypt, where he had fled from the face of King Solomon) that Jeroboam returned from Egypt.
\v 3 And they sent and they called to him, and Jeroboam and all of Israel came. And they spoke to Rehoboam, saying,
\v 4 “Your father made our yoke hard. And now make {it} lighter than the hard service of your father and than his heavy yoke that he put on us, and we will serve you.”
\v 5 And he said to them, “Until three days, then return to me.” So the people went.
\p
\v 6 And King Rehoboam consulted with the elders who had been standing to the face of Solomon his father while he was alive, saying, “How are you advising to return a word to this people?”
\v 7 And they spoke to him, saying, “If you become good to this people and are favorable to them and speak good words to them, then they will be servants to you all of the days.”
\v 8 But he forsook the counsel of the elders that they had counseled him, and he consulted the young men who had grown up with him {who} were standing to his face.
\v 9 And he said to them, “How are you advising that we should return a word {to} this people who spoke to me saying, ‘Make lighter than the yoke that your father put on us’?”
\v 10 And the young men who had grown up with him spoke, saying, “Thus you shall say to the people who spoke to you, saying, ‘Your father made our yoke heavy, but you, make light from upon us.’ Thus you shall say to them, ‘My little finger {is} thicker than the loins of my father.
\v 11 And now, my father loaded a heavy yoke on you, but I, I will add to your yoke. My father disciplined you with whips, but I, with scorpions.’”
\p
\v 12 And Jeroboam and all of the people came to Rehoboam on the third day, just as the king had spoken, saying, “Return to me on the third day.”
\v 13 And the king answered them harshly, and King Rehoboam forsook the counsel of the elders.
\v 14 And he spoke to them according to the counsel of the young men, saying, “I will make your yoke heavy, and I, I will add to it. My father disciplined you with whips, but I, with scorpions.”
\m
\v 15 And the king did not listen to the people, for the turn was from with God, so that Yahweh might raise up his word, which he had spoken by the hand of Ahijah the Shilonite to Jeroboam, the son of Nebat.
\p
\v 16 And all of Israel that the king had not listened to them, and the people returned {a word to} the king, saying, “What portion {is} to us in David? And no inheritance in the son of Jesse! A man to your tents, Israel! Now see your house, David!” And all of Israel went to its tents.
\v 17 (But the sons of Israel dwelling in the cities of Judah, Rehoboam reigned over them.)
\v 18 And King Rehoboam sent Hadoram, who {was} over the forced labor, but the sons of Israel stoned him {with} stone, and he died. And King Rehoboam strengthened himself to go up into the chariot to flee to Jerusalem.
\v 19 And Israel has rebelled against the house of David to this day.
\c 11
\p
\v 1 And Rehoboam entered Jerusalem, and he assembled the house of Judah and Benjamin, 180,000 chosen makers of war, to fight with Israel to return the kingdom to Rehoboam.
\v 2 But the word of Yahweh was to Shemaiah the man of God, saying,
\v 3 “Speak to Rehoboam, the son of Solomon, the king of Judah, and to all of Israel in Judah and Benjamin, saying,
\v 4 ‘Thus says Yahweh, “You shall not go up, and you shall not fight with your brothers. Return, a man to his house, for this thing has been from with me.”’” And they heard the words of Yahweh, and they returned from going against Jeroboam.
\p
\v 5 And Rehoboam dwelled in Jerusalem, and he built cities for fortification in Judah.
\v 6 And he built Bethlehem and Etam and Tekoa
\v 7 and Beth Zur and Soko and Adullam
\v 8 and Gath and Maresha, and Ziph
\v 9 and Adoraim and Lachish and Azekah
\v 10 and Zorah and Aijalon and Hebron, which {were} in Judah and in Benjamin, cities of fortifications.
\v 11 And he strengthened the fortifications, and he placed in them leaders and storehouses of food and oil and wine,
\v 12 and in each city and city, large shields and spears, and he strengthened them to much abundance. And Judah and Benjamin {were} to him.
\p
\v 13 And the priests and the Levites who {were} in all of Israel presented themselves to him from all of their borders.
\v 14 (For the Levites abandoned their pasturelands and their holding, and they went to Judah and to Jerusalem, for Jeroboam and his sons had rejected them from serving as priests to Yahweh.
\v 15 And he established for himself priests for the high places and for the goats and for the calves that he had made.)
\v 16 And after them, from all of the tribes of Israel, the ones giving their heart to seek Yahweh, the God of Israel, came {to} Jerusalem to sacrifice to Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\v 17 And they strengthened the kingdom of Judah, and they made Rehoboam, the son of Solomon, strong for three years, for they walked in the way of David and Solomon for three years.
\p
\v 18 And Rehoboam took a wife for himself, Mahalath, the daughter of Jerimoth, the son of David, {and of} Abihail, the daughter of Eliab, the son of Jesse.
\v 19 And she bore sons to him, Jeush and Shemariah and Zaham.
\v 20 And after her, he took Maacah, the daughter of Absalom, and she bore to him Abijah and Attai and Ziza and Shelomith.
\v 21 And Rehoboam loved Maacah, the daughter of Absalom, more than all of his wives and his concubines, for he took up 18 wives and 60 concubines, and he fathered 28 sons and 60 daughters.
\v 22 And Rehoboam established as head Abijah, the son of Maacah, as the leader among his brothers, in order to make him king.
\v 23 And he dealt wisely, and he scattered from all of his sons to all of the lands of Judah and Benjamin, to all of the cities of fortifications. And he gave provisions to them in abundance, and he asked for a multitude of wives.
\c 12
\p
\v 1 And it happened, at the establishing of the kingdom of Rehoboam and when {he was} strong, he abandoned the law of Yahweh, and all of Israel with him.
\v 2 And it happened in the fifth year of King Rehoboam, Shishak, the king of Egypt, came up against Jerusalem, because they had transgressed against Yahweh,
\v 3 with 1,200 chariots and with 60,000 horsemen, and there was no number for the people who came with him from Egypt: Libyans, Sukkites, and Cushites.
\v 4 And he captured the cities of fortification that {were} to Judah, and he came to Jerusalem.
\v 5 And Shemaiah the prophet came to Rehoboam and the princes of Judah who had assembled themselves to Jerusalem from the face of Shishak. And he said to them, “Thus says Yahweh, ‘You, you have abandoned me, so also I, I have abandoned you into the hand of Shishak.’”
\v 6 And the princes of Israel and the king humbled themselves. And they said, “Yahweh {is} righteous.”
\v 7 And when Yahweh saw that they had humbled themselves, the word of Yahweh was to Shemaiah, saying, “They have humbled themselves. I will not destroy them, and I will give to them like a little for an escape, and my heat will not pour onto Jerusalem by the hand of Shishak.
\v 8 But they will be to him for servants, and they will know my service and the service of the kingdoms of the lands.”
\p
\v 9 And Shishak, the king of Egypt, came up against Jerusalem. And he took the treasures of the house of Yahweh and the treasures of the house of the king. He took everything. And he took the shields of gold that Solomon had made.
\v 10 And King Rehoboam made shields of bronze in their place, and he committed {them} into the hand of the commanders of the runners, the ones keeping the entrance of the house of the king.
\v 11 And it happened, whenever the king entered the house of Yahweh, the runners came and bore them, and they brought them back to the chamber of the runners.
\v 12 And when he humbled himself, the nose of Yahweh turned from him, and did not destroy to completion. For good matters were also in Judah.
\p
\v 13 And King Rehoboam strengthened himself in Jerusalem, and he reigned. For Rehoboam {was} a son of 41 years when he reigned, and he reigned 17 years in Jerusalem, the city that Yahweh had chosen to put his name there from all of the tribes of Israel. And the name of his mother {was} Naamah the Ammonitess.
\v 14 And he did evil, for he did not establish his heart to seek Yahweh.
\p
\v 15 Now the matters of Rehoboam, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, are they not written in the words of Shemaiah the prophet and Iddo the seer to enroll? And the wars of Rehoboam and Jeroboam {were} all of the days.
\v 16 And Rehoboam lay down with his fathers, and he was buried in the City of David. And Abijah his son reigned in his place.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 In the year of 18 to King Jeroboam, Abijah reigned over Judah.
\v 2 He reigned three years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Macaiah, the daughter of Uriel, from Gibeah. And war was between Abijah and between Jeroboam.
\v 3 And Abijah bound the battle with an army {of} warriors of battle, 400,000 chosen men. And Jeroboam arrayed against him in battle 800,000 chosen men, a warrior of valor.
\v 4 And Abijah arose upon Mount Zemaraim, which {is} in the hill country of Ephraim, and he said, “Hear me, Jeroboam and all of Israel!
\v 5 {Is it} not to you to know that Yahweh, the God of Israel, gave the kingdom to David over Israel to eternity, to him and to his sons, a covenant of salt?
\v 6 But Jeroboam, the son of Nebat, the servant of Solomon, the son of David, arose, and he rebelled against his master.
\v 7 And worthless men, sons of wickedness, gathered themselves to him, and they strengthened themselves against Rehoboam, the son of Solomon. And Rehoboam was young and tender of heart, and he did not strengthen himself to their faces.
\v 8 And now, you are saying to strengthen yourselves to the face of the kingdom of Yahweh in the hand of the sons of David, and you {are} a great multitude, and with you {are} the calves of gold that Jeroboam has made for you for gods.
\v 9 Have you not driven out the priests of Yahweh, the sons of Aaron, and the Levites, and made for yourselves priests like the peoples of the lands? Any who comes to fill his hand with a bull, a son of the herd, and seven rams, then he will be a priest for not-gods.
\v 10 But we, Yahweh {is} our God, and we have not abandoned him. And priests are ministering to Yahweh, sons of Aaron, and Levites {are} in the work.
\v 11 And they are sacrificing burnt offerings to Yahweh by morning, by morning and by evening, by evening, with incense of spices. And the arrangement of bread {is} on the pure table, and the lampstand of gold and its lamps, to burn evening by evening. For we are keeping the keeping of Yahweh, our God, but you, you have abandoned him.
\v 12 And behold with us, at the head, God and his priests and the trumpets of the shout to make a shout against you. Sons of Israel, do not fight with Yahweh, the God of your fathers, for you will not prosper.”
\p
\v 13 But Jeroboam had made an ambush circle to come from behind them, and they were to the face of Judah, and the ambush {was} from behind them.
\v 14 And Judah turned, and behold, the battle {was} to them before and behind. And they cried out to Yahweh, and the priests were blowing on trumpets.
\v 15 And they shouted, a man of Judah, and it happened when a man of Judah shouted, that God struck Jeroboam and all of Israel to the face of Abijah and Judah.
\v 16 And the sons of Israel fled from the face of Judah, and God gave them into their hand.
\v 17 And Abijah and his people struck them a great striking, and they fell slain, 500,000 chosen men from Israel.
\v 18 And the sons of Israel were humbled at that time, and the sons of Judah were strong because they relied themselves on Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\v 19 And Abijah pursued after Jeroboam, and he captured cities from him: Bethel and its daughters and Jeshanah and its daughters and Ephron and its daughters.
\v 20 And the strength of Jeroboam did not retain again in the days of Abijah. And Yahweh struck him, and he died.
\v 21 And Abijah strengthened himself; and he took up for himself 14 wives, and he fathered 22 sons and 16 daughters.
\v 22 Now the rest of the matters of Abijah and his ways and his words {are} written in the treatise of the prophet Iddo.
\c 14
\p
\v 1 And Abijah lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in the City of David. And Asa his son reigned in his place. In his days the land rested ten years.
\v 2 And Asa did the good and the right in the eyes of Yahweh his God.
\v 3 And he removed the altars of the foreigner and the high places, and he shattered the pillars, and he cut down the Asherahs.
\v 4 And he said to Judah to seek Yahweh, the God of their fathers, and to do the law and the commandment.
\v 5 And he removed the high places and the sun images from all of the cities of Judah. And the kingdom rested to his face.
\v 6 And he built cities of fortification in Judah, for the land rested. And war was not with him in these years, because Yahweh had given rest to him.
\v 7 And he said to Judah, “Let us build these cities, and let us surround {them} {with} a wall and towers, doors and bars. It is still to our faces, the land, because we have sought Yahweh our God. We have sought, and he has given rest to us from all around.” And they built, and they prospered.
\v 8 And an army was to Asa: from Judah, 300,000 carrying the large shield and the spear, and from Benjamin, 280,000 carrying the shield and treading the bow. All of these were warriors of valor.
\p
\v 9 Then Zerah the Cushite came out against them with an army {of} a thousand thousands and 300 chariots, and he came to Mareshah.
\v 10 And Asa went out to his face, and they arrayed {for} battle in the Valley of Zephathah at Mareshah.
\v 11 And Asa called to Yahweh his God and said, “Yahweh, {it is} nothing with you to help between many and to {one} without strength. Help us, Yahweh our God, for we rely ourselves on you, and in your name we have come against this multitude. Yahweh, you are our God. do not let man retain {strength} against you.”
\v 12 And Yahweh struck the Cushites to the face of Asa and to the face of Judah, and the Cushites fled.
\v 13 And Asa and the people who {were} with him pursued them until to Gerar. And one fell from the Cushites until there was not preservation to them, for they were broken to the face of Yahweh and to the face of his camp. And they carried away very much plunder.
\v 14 And they struck all of the cities around Gerar, for the fear of Yahweh was upon them. And they despoiled all of the cities, for much spoil was in them.
\v 15 And they also struck the tents of livestock, and they captured sheep in abundance and camels, and they returned {to} Jerusalem.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 And Azariah, the son of Oded, the Spirit of God was upon him.
\v 2 And he went out to the face of Asa, and he said to him, “Hear me, Asa, and all of Judah and Benjamin! Yahweh {is} with you when you are with him. And if you seek him, he will be found by you, but if you abandon him, he will abandon you.
\v 3 And many days {were} to Israel without the true God and without a priest teaching and without the law.
\v 4 But in the distress {that was} to them, they turned to Yahweh, the God of Israel, and they sought him, and he was found by them.
\v 5 And in those times, there was no peace for going out and for coming in, for great turmoil {was} upon all of the dwellers of the lands.
\v 6 And they were crushed, nation by nation and city by city, for God troubled them with every distress.
\v 7 But you, be strong, and let your hands not slacken, for there will be a reward for your work.”
\p
\v 8 And when Asa heard these words and the prophecy {of} Oded the prophet, he strengthened himself, and he made the abominations pass away from all of the land of Judah and Benjamin and from the cities that he had captured from the hill country of Ephraim. And he repaired the altar of Yahweh that {was} to the face of the porch of Yahweh.
\v 9 And he gathered all of Judah and Benjamin and the sojourners with them from Ephraim and Manasseh and from Simeon, for they had fallen to him from Israel in abundance when they saw that Yahweh his God {was} with him.
\v 10 And they gathered {at} Jerusalem in the third month in the year of 15 to the reign of Asa.
\v 11 And they sacrificed to Yahweh in that day from the plunder they had brought 700 oxen and 7,000 sheep.
\v 12 And they entered into a covenant to seek Yahweh, the God of their fathers, with all of their heart and with all of their soul.
\v 13 And any who did not seek Yahweh, the God of Israel, would be killed, from small and to great, from man and to woman.
\v 14 And they swore to Yahweh with a great voice and with a shout and with trumpets and with shofars.
\v 15 And all of Judah rejoiced about the oath, for with all of their heart they had sworn and with all of their desire they had sought him, and he had been found by them. And Yahweh gave rest to them from around.
\p
\v 16 And also Maacah, the mother of King Asa, he removed her from {being} queen mother because she had made a horror for Asherah. And Asa cut down her horror, and he crushed {it} and burned {it} in the wadi of Kidron.
\v 17 But the high places, they did not remove from Israel. Nevertheless, the heart of Asa was whole all of his days.
\v 18 And he brought the holy things of his father and his holy things {into} the house of God, silver and gold and vessels.
\v 19 And war was not until the year of 35 to the reign of Asa.
\c 16
\p
\v 1 In the year of 36 to the reign of Asa, Baasha, the king of Israel, went up against Judah, and he built Ramah so as not to give going out or coming in to Asa, the king of Judah.
\v 2 And Asa brought out the silver and the gold from the storerooms of the house of Yahweh and the house of the king, and he sent {them} to Ben Hadad, the king of Aram, the dweller in Damascus, saying,
\v 3 “A covenant between me and between you, and between my father and between your father. Behold, I have sent silver and gold to you. Go, break your covenant with Baasha, the king of Israel, that he may go up from against me.”
\v 4 And Ben Hadad listened to King Asa, and he sent the commanders of the armies that {were} to him against the cities of Israel. And they struck Ijon and Dan and Abel Maim and all of the storehouses of the cities of Naphtali.
\v 5 And it happened, when Baasha heard, that he ceased from building Ramah, and he stopped his work.
\v 6 And King Asa took all of Judah, and they carried away the stones of Ramah and its wood {with} which Baasha had been building, and he built with them Geba and Mizpah.
\p
\v 7 And at that time Hanani the seer came to Asa, the king of Judah, and he said to him, “Because you relied yourself on the king of Aram, and you did not rely yourself on Yahweh your God, therefore the army of the king of Aram has escaped from your hand.
\v 8 Were not the Cushites and the Lubites an army to abundance, with very many chariots and horsemen? But because you relied yourself on Yahweh, he gave them into your hand.
\v 9 For Yahweh, his eyes are going about in all of the earth to strengthen himself with {those} whose heart {is} whole toward him. You have acted foolishly regarding this. For from now, there will be wars with you.”
\v 10 And Asa was angry with the seer, and he put him {in} the house of the stocks, for {he was} in rage with him regarding this. And Asa oppressed some of the people at that time.
\p
\v 11 And behold, the matters of Asa, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, behold them written on the scroll of the kings of Judah and Israel.
\v 12 And Asa became sick in the year of 39 to his reign, in his feet, unto the increasing of his sickness. But even in his sickness, he did not seek Yahweh, but among the healers.
\v 13 And Asa lay down with his fathers, and he died in the year of 41 to his reign.
\v 14 And they buried him in his graves that he had dug out for himself in the City of David. And they laid him on a bed that one filled with spices and kinds {of} mixtures with an ointment of work. And they burned for him a burning great to much.
\c 17
\p
\v 1 And Jehoshaphat his son reigned in his place, and he strengthened himself against Israel.
\v 2 And he put a force in all of the fortified cities of Judah, and he put garrisons in the land of Judah and in the cities of Ephraim that Asa his father had captured.
\v 3 And Yahweh was with Jehoshaphat because he walked in the former ways of David his father and he did not seek the Baals.
\v 4 Instead, he sought the God of his father, and he walked in his commandments and not according to the work of Israel.
\v 5 And Yahweh established the kingdom in his hand, and all of Judah gave tribute to Jehoshaphat. And wealth was to him, and glory in abundance.
\v 6 And his heart was high in the ways of Yahweh. And he even removed the high places and the Asherahs from Judah.
\p
\v 7 And in the year of three to his reign, he sent his officials, Ben Hail and Obadiah and Zechariah and Nethanel and Micaiah, to teach in the cities of Judah,
\v 8 and with them, the Levites, Shemaiah and Nethaniah and Zebadiah and Asahel and Shemiramoth and Jehonathan and Adonijah and Tobijah and Tob Adonijah, the Levites, and with them, Elishama and Jehoram, the priests.
\v 9 And they taught in Judah, and the scroll of the law of Yahweh {was} with them. And they went around in all of the cities of Judah, and they taught the people.
\p
\v 10 And the fear of Yahweh was on all of the kingdoms of the lands that {were} around Judah, and they did not fight with Jehoshaphat.
\v 11 And some of the Philistines were bringing a gift to Jehoshaphat and a silver burden. Also, the Arabians were bringing flocks to him, 7,700 rams and 7,700 male goats.
\v 12 And it happened, Jehoshaphat was going and becoming great unto upward. And he built fortresses and cities of storehouses in Judah.
\v 13 And much work was to him in the cities of Judah and men of battle, warriors of valor, in Jerusalem.
\v 14 And these {were} their muster according to the house of their fathers. For Judah, commanders of thousands, Adnah, the commander, and with him 300,000 warriors of valor,
\v 15 and at his hand Jehohanan the commander, and with him 280,000,
\v 16 and at his hand Amasiah, the son of Zichri, one having volunteered himself to Yahweh, and with him 200,000 warriors of valor.
\v 17 And from Benjamin, a warrior of valor, Eliada, and with him 200,000 kissers of the bow and shield,
\v 18 and at his hand Jehozabad, and with him 180,000 equipped for war.
\v 19 These were serving the king, besides {those} whom the king put in the cities of fortification in all of Judah.
\c 18
\p
\v 1 And wealth was to Jehoshaphat, and glory in abundance. And he made a marriage alliance with Ahab.
\v 2 And he went down at the end of some years to Ahab in Samaria, and Ahab sacrificed sheep and cattle in abundance for him and for the people who {were} with him. And he incited him to go up against Ramoth Gilead.
\v 3 And Ahab, the king of Israel, said to Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, “Will you go with me {to} Ramoth Gilead?” And he said to him, “Like me, like you, and like your people, my people, and with you in battle.”
\p
\v 4 And Jehoshaphat said to the king of Israel, “Please seek as today the word of Yahweh.”
\v 5 And the king of Israel gathered the prophets, 400 men, and he said to them, “Shall we go to Ramoth Gilead for battle, or shall I refrain?” And they said, “Go up, and God will give {it} into the hand of the king.”
\v 6 And Jehoshaphat said, “{Is} no prophet of Yahweh still here, that we may seek from him?”
\v 7 And the king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, “{There is} still one man for seeking Yahweh from him, but I, I hate him because he is not prophesying good about me but, all of his days, evil. He {is} Micaiah, the son of Imlah.” And Jehoshaphat said, “May the king not say thus.”
\v 8 So the king of Israel called to a eunuch and said, “Hasten Micaiah, the son of Imlah.”
\v 9 Now the king of Israel and Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, were sitting, a man on his throne, clothed {in} robes. And they were sitting by the threshing floor {at} the entrance of the gate of Samaria. And all of the prophets were prophesying to their faces.
\v 10 And Zedekiah, the son of Kenaanah, made horns of iron for himself, and he said, “Thus says Yahweh: ‘With these you will gore Aram until their finishing.’”
\v 11 And all of the prophets were prophesying thus, saying, “Go up {to} Ramoth Gilead and prosper, for Yahweh will give {it} into the hand of the king.”
\p
\v 12 And the messenger who went to call Micaiah spoke to him, saying, “Behold, the words of the prophets {with} one mouth {are} good for the king. Please let your word be like one from them, and may you speak good.”
\v 13 And Micaiah said, “The life of Yahweh, surely what my God says, I will speak it.”
\v 14 And he came to the king, and the king said to him, “Micaiah, shall we go to Ramoth Gilead for battle, or shall I refrain?” And he said, “Go up and prosper, for he will give {it} into your hand.”
\v 15 And the king said to him, “Up to how many times have I been making you swear that you would not speak about me except truth in the name of Yahweh?”
\v 16 And he said, “I saw all of Israel scattered on the hills like a flock when no shepherd {is} to them. And Yahweh said, ‘No masters {are} to these. Let them return, a man to his house, in peace.’”
\v 17 And the king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, “Did I not say to you, ‘He will not prophesy good about me, but rather evil’?”
\v 18 And he said, “Therefore, hear the word of Yahweh. I saw Yahweh sitting on his throne, and all of the army of the heavens was standing to his right and his left.
\v 19 And Yahweh said, ‘Who will deceive Ahab, the king of Israel, so that he will go up and fall at Ramoth Gilead?’ And one said; this {one} was saying, ‘By thus,’ and that {one} was saying, ‘By thus.’
\v 20 Then a spirit came out and stood to the face of Yahweh. And he said, ‘I, I will deceive {him}.’ And Yahweh said to him, ‘By what?’
\v 21 And he said, ‘I will go out and be a spirit of falsehood in the mouth of all of his prophets.’ And he said, ‘You will deceive and also you will be able. Go out and do thus.’
\v 22 And now, behold, Yahweh has put a spirit of falsehood into the mouth of these your prophets. For Yahweh has spoken harm against you.”
\p
\v 23 And Zedekiah, the son of Kenaanah, approached and struck Micaiah on the cheek. And he said, “Where {is} this way {by which} the Spirit of Yahweh has passed over from with me to speak to you?”
\v 24 And Micaiah said, “Behold you, seeing on that day when you enter a chamber in a chamber to hide yourself.”
\v 25 And the king of Israel said, “Take Micaiah and bring him back to Amon, the commander of the city, and to Joash, the son of the king.
\v 26 And you shall say, ‘Thus says the king, “Put this {one} in the house of imprisonment and feed him bread of affliction and water of affliction until my returning in peace.”’”
\v 27 And Micaiah said, “If returning, you return in peace, Yahweh has not spoken by me.” And he said, “Listen, peoples, all of them!”
\p
\v 28 And the king of Israel went up, and Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, {to} Ramoth Gilead.
\v 29 And the king of Israel said to Jehoshaphat, “I will disguise myself and come into the battle, but you, wear your robes.” And the king of Israel disguised himself, and they came into the battle.
\v 30 Now the king of Aram had commanded the commanders of the chariotry that {was} to him, saying, “You shall not fight small, great, but with the king of Israel alone.”
\v 31 And it happened, when the commanders of the chariotry saw Jehoshaphat, that they, they said, “He {is} the king of Israel!” And they turned aside to fight against him. And Jehoshaphat cried out, and Yahweh helped him, and God incited them from him.
\v 32 And it happened, when the commanders of the chariotry saw that he was not the king of Israel, that they turned back from after him.
\v 33 But a man drew the bow in his innocence, and he struck the king of Israel between the joints and between the armor. And he said to his charioteer, “Turn your hand and bring me out from the camp, for I am wounded.”
\v 34 And the battle went up on that day, and the king of Israel was propped up in the chariot opposite Aram until the evening. And he died at the time of the going in of the sun.
\c 19
\p
\v 1 And Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, returned to his house in peace, to Jerusalem.
\v 2 But Jehu, the son of Hanani, the seer, came out to his face, and he said to King Jehoshaphat, “Should you help the wicked and love the haters of Yahweh? And because of this, wrath {is} upon you from the face of Yahweh.
\v 3 Nevertheless, good things have been found with you, for you have consumed the Asherahs from the land, and you have established your heart to seek God.”
\p
\v 4 And Jehoshaphat dwelled in Jerusalem. And he returned and went out among the people, from Beersheba to the hill country of Ephraim. And he made them return to Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\v 5 And he appointed judges in the land in all of the fortified cities of Judah, for city and city.
\v 6 And he said to the judges, “See what you are doing. For you do not judge for man but for Yahweh; and with you in a matter of judgment.
\v 7 And now, let the fear of Yahweh be upon you. Watch and act, for with Yahweh our God there is no iniquity or lifting of faces or taking of a bribe.”
\p
\v 8 And also in Jerusalem Jehoshaphat appointed from the Levites and the priests and from the heads of fathers for Israel for the judgment of Yahweh and for dispute. (For they had returned to Jerusalem.)
\v 9 And he commanded them, saying, “Thus you shall do in the fear of Yahweh, in trustworthiness and with a whole heart.
\v 10 And any dispute that comes to you from your brothers, the ones dwelling in their cities, between blood to blood, between law to command, to statutes and to judgments, then you shall warn them, and they will not become guilty toward Yahweh and wrath will {not} be on you and on your brothers. Thus you shall do, and you will not be guilty.
\v 11 And behold, Amariah the head priest {is} over you for any matter of Yahweh, and Zebadiah, the son of Ishmael, the leader of the house of Judah, for any matter of the king. And the Levites {are} officers to your faces. Be strong and act, and may Yahweh be with the good!”
\c 20
\p
\v 1 And it happened after this, the sons of Moab and the sons of Ammon, and with them from the Ammonites, came against Jehoshaphat for battle.
\v 2 And they came and declared to Jehoshaphat, saying, “A great multitude is coming against you from beyond the sea, from Aram. And behold them in Hazezon Tamar (it {is} Engedi).
\v 3 And he feared, and Jehoshaphat set his face to seek Yahweh. And he proclaimed a fast over all of Judah.
\v 4 And Judah gathered to seek from Yahweh. Even from all of the cities of Judah, they came to seek Yahweh.
\p
\v 5 And Jehoshaphat stood in the assembly of Judah and Jerusalem, in the house of Yahweh, to the face of the new courtyard.
\v 6 And he said, “Yahweh, the God of our fathers, {are} you not he, the God in the heavens? And you are ruling over all of the kingdoms of the nations, and in your hand {are} power and might, and none stations himself with you.
\v 7 You, our God, did you not dispossess the dwellers of this land from the face of your people Israel and give it to the seed of Abraham your friend, to eternity?
\v 8 And they have dwelled in it, and they have built for you in it a sanctuary for your name, saying,
\v 9 ‘If harm comes upon us—the sword, judgment or pestilence or famine—we will stand to the face of this house and to your face, for your name {is} in this house, and we will cry out to you from our trouble, and may you hear, and may you save.’
\v 10 And now, behold, the sons of Ammon and Moab and Mount Seir, whom you did not give to Israel to enter against them when they came from the land of Egypt, for they turned from against them, and they did not destroy them,
\v 11 but behold them, repaying against us, coming to drive us out from your possession that you made us possess.
\v 12 Our God, will you not judge them? For there is not strength in us to the face of this great multitude coming against us. And we, we do not know what we should do, but our eyes {are} upon you.”
\v 13 And all of Judah was standing to the face of Yahweh, even their children, their wives, and their sons.
\p
\v 14 And Jahaziel, the son of Zechariah, the son of Benaiah, the son of Jeiel, the son of Mattaniah, a Levite from the sons of Asaph, the Spirit of Yahweh was upon him in the midst of the assembly.
\v 15 And he said, “Attend, all of Judah and dwellers of Jerusalem and King Jehoshaphat. Thus says Yahweh to you, ‘You, may you not fear and may you not be dismayed from the face of this great multitude, for the battle {is} not to you but to God.
\v 16 Tomorrow, go down against them. Behold them, coming up by the Ascent of Ziz. And you will find them at the end of the wadi, {to} the face of the wilderness of Jeruel.
\v 17 {It is} not to you to fight in this. Station yourselves, stand and see the salvation of Yahweh with you, Judah and Jerusalem. May you not fear and may you not be dismayed. Tomorrow, go out to their face, and Yahweh {will be} with you.’”
\v 18 And Jehoshaphat bowed down, nostrils earthward. And all of Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem fell to the face of Yahweh to prostrate themselves to Yahweh.
\v 19 And Levites from the sons of the Kohathites and from the sons of the Korahites arose to praise Yahweh, the God of Israel, with a great voice to upward.
\p
\v 20 And they got up early in the morning, and they went out to the wilderness of Tekoa. And when they went out, Jehoshaphat stood and said, “Hear me, Judah and dwellers of Jerusalem!
\q1 Believe in Yahweh your God, and you will be confirmed.
\q2 Believe in his prophets, and you will succeed.”
\m
\v 21 And he consulted with the people, and he stationed the singers to Yahweh and the praisers of the beauty of holiness in going out to the face of the equipped. And they were saying, “Thank Yahweh, for his covenant faithfulness {is} to eternity.”
\v 22 And at the time they began with a joyful shout and praise, Yahweh put ambushes against the sons of Ammon, Moab, and Mount Seir coming to Judah, and they were struck.
\v 23 For the sons of Ammon and Moab stood against the dwellers of Mount Seir, to devote and to destroy {them}. And when they finished with the dwellers of Seir, they helped, a man against his neighbor, for destruction.
\p
\v 24 And Judah came to the watchtower in the wilderness, and they turned toward the multitude, and behold, corpses fallen earthward! And there was no escape.
\v 25 And Jehoshaphat and his people came to plunder their spoil, and they found among them in abundance both property and corpses and vessels of delight. And they delivered for themselves to no carrying away. And they were three days plundering the spoil, for it {was} great.
\v 26 And on the fourth day they assembled in the Valley of Beracah. For they blessed Yahweh there, therefore they called the name of that place the Valley of Beracah, to today.
\v 27 And they returned, every man of Judah and Jerusalem, and Jehoshaphat {was} at their head, to return to Jerusalem with rejoicing, for Yahweh had made them rejoice over their enemies.
\v 28 And they came to Jerusalem with lyres and with harps and with trumpets to the house of Yahweh.
\v 29 And the fear of God {was} upon all of the kingdoms of the lands when they heard that Yahweh had fought with the enemies of Israel.
\v 30 And the kingdom of Jehoshaphat rested, for his God gave rest to him from around.
\p
\v 31 And Jehoshaphat reigned over Judah. {He was} a son of 35 years when he reigned, and he reigned 25 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Azubah, the daughter of Shilhi.
\v 32 And he walked in the way of Asa his father, and he did not turn from it, doing right in the eyes of Yahweh.
\v 33 However, they did not remove the high places. And the people still did not establish their heart to the God of their fathers.
\v 34 Now the rest of the matters of Jehoshaphat, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, behold them, written in the words of Jehu, the son of Hanani, which is brought up on the scroll of the kings of Israel.
\p
\v 35 And after this, Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, allied himself with Ahaziah, the king of Israel. He, he acted wickedly by doing {this}.
\v 36 And he allied himself with him to make ships to go {to} Tarshish. And they made the ships at Ezion Geber.
\v 37 But Eliezer, the son of Dodavahu from Mareshah, prophesied against Jehoshaphat, saying, “When you allied yourself with Ahaziah, Yahweh broke through your works.” And the ships were broken, and they were not able to go to Tarshish.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 And Jehoshaphat lay down with his fathers, and he was buried with his fathers in the City of David. And Jehoram his son reigned in his place.
\v 2 Now brothers {were} to him, the sons of Jehoshaphat: Azariah and Jehiel and Zechariah and Azariahu and Michael and Shephatiah. All of these {were} sons of Jehoshaphat, the king of Israel.
\v 3 And their father gave to them many gifts of silver and of gold and of valuables, with cities of fortification in Judah. But the kingdom, he gave to Jehoram, for he {was} the firstborn.
\v 4 And Jehoram arose over the kingdom of his father, and he strengthened himself. And he killed all of his brothers with the sword, and also some of the princes of Israel.
\v 5 Jehoram {was} a son of 32 years when he reigned, and he reigned 8 years in Jerusalem.
\v 6 And he walked in the way of the kings of Israel, just as the house of Ahab did, for a daughter of Ahab was a wife to him. And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh.
\v 7 But Yahweh was not willing to destroy the house of David because of the covenant that he had cut with David, and just as he had said, to give a lamp to him and to his sons all of the days.
\p
\v 8 In his days, Edom rebelled from under the hand of Judah, and they made a king rule over them.
\v 9 So Joram passed over with his commanders and all of the chariotry with him. And it happened, he arose at night, and he struck Edom having surrounded to him and the commanders of the chariotry.
\v 10 So Edom has rebelled from under the hand of Judah to this day. Then Libnah rebelled at that time from under his hand, for he had abandoned Yahweh, the God of his fathers.
\p
\v 11 Also, he, he made high places in the hills of Judah, and he made the dwellers of Jerusalem whore, and he drove Judah away.
\v 12 And a writing came to him from Elijah the prophet, saying, “Thus says Yahweh, the God of David your father, ‘Because you have not walked in the ways of Jehoshaphat your father and in the ways of Asa, the king of Judah,
\v 13 but you have walked in the way of the kings of Israel, and you have made Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem whore like the whoring of the house of Ahab, and also, you have killed your brothers, the house of your father, the ones better than you,
\v 14 behold Yahweh, striking a great striking among your people and among your sons and among your wives and among all of your property
\v 15 and you with many sicknesses, with a disease of your intestines, until your intestines come out from the sickness, days upon days.’”
\p
\v 16 And Yahweh awakened against Jehoram the spirit of the Philistines and the Arabians who {were} at the hand of the Cushites,
\v 17 and they came up against Judah, and they broke through it. And they carried away all of the property found in the house of the king, and also his sons and his wives, and a son was not left to him except Jehoahaz, the youngest of his sons.
\p
\v 18 And after all of this, Yahweh struck him in his intestines with a sickness to no cure.
\v 19 And it happened to days from days, that at the time of the going out of the end of two years, his intestines came out because of his sickness, and he died with bad maladies. And his people did not make for him a burning like the burning of his fathers.
\v 20 He was a son of 32 {years} when he reigned, and he reigned eight years in Jerusalem, and he went with no desire. And they buried him in the City of David, but not in the graves of the kings.
\c 22
\p
\v 1 And the dwellers of Jerusalem made Ahaziah, his youngest son, reign in his place, for the band having come with the Arabians into the camp had killed all of the older. And Ahaziah, the son of Jehoram, the king of Judah, reigned.
\v 2 Ahaziah {was} a son of 42 years when he reigned, and he reigned one year in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Athaliah, the daughter of Omri.
\v 3 He, he also walked in the ways of the house of Ahab, for his mother was his counselor to act wickedly.
\v 4 And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh, like the house of Ahab, for they, they were counselors to him after the death of his father, for destruction to him.
\v 5 He also walked in their counsel, and he went with Jehoram, the son of Ahab, the king of Israel, to war against Hazael, the king of Aram, at Ramoth Gilead. And the Arameans struck Joram.
\v 6 And he returned to be healed in Jezreel because of the strikings that they had struck him at Ramah when he fought with Hazael, the king of Aram. And Ahaziah, the son of Jehoram, the king of Judah, went down to see Jehoram, the son of Ahab, in Jezreel because he was infirm.
\p
\v 7 And the destruction of Ahaziah was from God, to go to Joram. For when he came, he went out with Jehoram against Jehu, the son of Nimshi, whom Yahweh had anointed him to cut off the house of Ahab.
\v 8 And it happened, when Jehu was judging with the house of Ahab, that he found the princes of Judah and the sons of the brothers of Ahaziah, ones ministering to Ahaziah, and he killed them.
\v 9 And he sought Ahaziah; and they captured him (and he had been hiding himself in Samaria). And they brought him to Jehu, and they killed him. And they buried him, for they said, “He {is} the son of Jehoshaphat, who sought Yahweh with all of his heart.” And there was none to the house of Ahaziah to retain strength for the kingdom.
\p
\v 10 And Athaliah, the mother of Ahaziah, saw that her son had died. And she arose, and she destroyed all of the seed of the kingdom of the house of Judah.
\v 11 But Jehoshabeath, a daughter of the king, took Joash, the son of Ahaziah, and she stole him away from the midst of the sons of the king being killed, and she put him and his nurse in the room of the beds. And Jehoshabeath, the daughter of king Jehoram, the wife of Jehoiada the priest (for she was the sister of Ahaziah) hid him from the face of Athaliah, and she did not kill him.
\v 12 And he was with them in the house of God, being hidden six years. And Athaliah was reigning over the land.
\c 23
\p
\v 1 And in the seventh year, Jehoiada strengthened himself, and he took the commanders of hundreds, Azariah, the son of Jehoram, and Ishmael, the son of Jehohanan, and Azariah, the son of Obed, and Maaseiah, the son of Adaiah, and Elishaphat, the son of Zichri, with him into a covenant.
\v 2 And they went around in Judah, and they gathered the Levites from all of the cities of Judah and the heads of the fathers of Israel. And they came to Jerusalem.
\v 3 And all of the assembly cut a covenant in the house of God with the king. And he said to them, “Behold, the son of the king will reign, just as Yahweh spoke about the sons of David.
\v 4 This {is} the thing that you shall do. A third of you coming in on the Sabbath from the priests and from the Levites {shall be} keepers of the thresholds,
\v 5 and a third {shall be} at the house of the king, and a third {shall be} at the Gate of the Foundation. And all of the people {shall be} in the courtyards of the house of Yahweh.
\v 6 And let not one enter the house of Yahweh except the priests and the ministering ones of the Levites. They, they may enter, for they {are} holy. And all of the people shall keep the keeping of Yahweh.
\v 7 And the Levites shall surround the king around, a man with his weapons in his hand, and one entering into the house shall be killed. And they will be with the king when he comes in and when he goes out.”
\p
\v 8 And the Levites and all of Judah did according to all that Jehoiada the priest commanded. And they took, a man his men, the ones coming in on the Sabbath with the ones going out on the Sabbath, for Jehoiada the priest did not dismiss the divisions.
\v 9 And Jehoiada the priest gave to the commanders of hundreds the spears and the shields and the large shields that {had been} to King David that {were in} the house of God.
\v 10 And he stationed all of the people, and a man {had} his weapon in his hand, from the right shoulder of the house to the left shoulder of the house, to the altar and to the house, by the king all around.
\v 11 And they brought out the son of the king, and they put a crown on him, and the testimony, and they made him reign, and Jehoiada and his sons anointed him. And they said, “May the king live!”
\p
\v 12 And Athaliah heard the sound of the people running and praising the king, and she came to the people {at} the house of Yahweh.
\v 13 And she looked, and behold, the king was standing by the pillar at the entrance, and the commanders and the trumpeters {were} by the king, and all of the people of the land were rejoicing and blasting on trumpets, and the singers with instruments of song and teaching to praise. And Athaliah tore her clothes, and she said, “Conspiracy! Conspiracy!”
\v 14 And Jehoiada the priest brought out the commanders of hundreds, the officers of the army, and he said to them, “Bring her out from the house to the ranks. And the one coming after her, let him be killed with the sword.” For the priest said, “May you not kill her {in} the house of Yahweh.”
\v 15 And they put two hands to her, and she came to the entrance of the gate of horses {of} the house of the king, and they killed her there.
\p
\v 16 And Jehoiada cut a covenant between himself and between all of the people and between the king to become a people for Yahweh.
\v 17 And all of the people entered the house of Baal, and they tore it down, and they shattered his altars and his images, and they killed Mattan, the priest of Baal, to the face of the altars.
\v 18 And Jehoiada set the appointments of the house of Yahweh by the hand of the priests, the Levites whom David had assigned over the house of Yahweh, to offer the burnt offerings of Yahweh, as written in the law of Moses, with rejoicing and with song by the hands of David.
\v 19 And he stationed gatekeepers at the gates of the house of Yahweh, that the unclean should not enter in any way.
\v 20 And he took the commanders of hundreds and the nobles and the rulers of the people and all of the people of the land, and they brought the king down from the house of Yahweh, and they entered in the midst of the Upper Gate {of} the house of the king. And they sat the king on the throne of the kingdom.
\v 21 And all of the people of the land rejoiced, and the city rested, for they had killed Athaliah with the sword.
\c 24
\p
\v 1 Joash {was} a son of seven years when he reigned. And he reigned 40 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother was Zibiah, from Beersheba.
\v 2 And Joash did right in the eyes of Yahweh all of the days of Jehoiada the priest.
\v 3 And Jehoiada took up two wives for him, and he fathered sons and daughters.
\p
\v 4 And it happened after this, it was with the heart of Joash to repair the house of Yahweh.
\v 5 And he gathered the priests and the Levites, and he said to them, “Go out to the cities of Judah and gather from all of Israel enough silver to strengthen the house of your God year by year. And you, hasten to the matter.” But the Levites did not hasten.
\v 6 And the king called for Jehoiada, the head, and he said to him, “Why have you not sought after the Levites to bring in from Judah and from Jerusalem the tribute of Moses the servant of Yahweh and the assembly of Israel for the tent of the testimony?”
\v 7 For the wicked Athaliah, her sons had broken into the house of God, and they had also used all of the sacred {things} of the house of Yahweh for the Baals.
\p
\v 8 And the king spoke, and they made a chest, and they placed it by the gate of the house of Yahweh, outside.
\v 9 And they gave voice in Judah and in Jerusalem to bring in to Yahweh the tribute of Moses the servant of God over Israel in the wilderness.
\v 10 And all of the princes and all of the people rejoiced, and they brought in and they threw into the chest until to completion.
\v 11 And it happened, at the time {when} one brought in the chest to the oversight of the king by the hand of the Levites, that when they saw that {there was} an abundance of silver, that a scribe of the king would come and an officer of the head priest. And they would empty the chest, and they would carry it and bring it back to its place. Thus they did to day by day, and they gathered silver to abundance.
\v 12 And the king and Jehoiada gave it to the doers of the work of the service of the house of Yahweh. And they were hiring stonecutters and craftsmen to repair the house of Yahweh, and also for craftsmen of iron and bronze to strengthen the house of Yahweh.
\v 13 And the doers of the work did, and the health of the work went up by their hand. And they made the house of God stand according to its measurement, and they strengthened it.
\v 14 And when they finished, they brought the remainder of the silver to the face of the king and Jehoiada. And they made vessels for the house of Yahweh, items of ministry and burnt offerings and palms and vessels of gold and silver. And they were offering up burnt offerings at the house of Yahweh continually all of the days of Jehoiada.
\p
\v 15 And Jehoiada aged and became full {of} days, and he died, a son of 130 years at his death.
\v 16 And they buried him in the City of David with the kings, for he had done good in Israel and with God and his house.
\v 17 And after the death of Jehoiada, the princes of Judah came and prostrated themselves to the king. Then the king listened to them.
\v 18 And they abandoned the house of Yahweh, the God of their fathers, and they served Asherahs and idols. And wrath was upon Judah and Jerusalem for this their guilt.
\v 19 And he sent prophets among them to bring them back to Yahweh, and they testified against them, but they did not listen.
\p
\v 20 And the Spirit of God clothed Zechariah, the son of Jehoiada the priest. And he stood above the people, and he said to them, “Thus says God, ‘Why are you transgressing the commands of Yahweh, that you do not prosper? Because you have abandoned Yahweh, so he has abandoned you.”
\v 21 But they conspired against him, and they stoned him {with} stone by the command of the king in the courtyard of the house of Yahweh.
\v 22 And King Joash did not remember the covenant faithfulness that Jehoiada, his father, did with him, and he killed his son. And as he died, he said, “May Yahweh see and seek.”
\p
\v 23 And it happened at the turn of the year, the army of Aram came up against him, and they came to Judah and Jerusalem. And they destroyed all of the princes of the people from the people. And they sent all of the plunder to the king of Damascus.
\v 24 Although the army of Aram came with a small amount of men, yet Yahweh gave a very large army into their hand because they had abandoned Yahweh, the God of their fathers. So they did judgment on Joash.
\p
\v 25 And when they went from him (for they had left him with many wounds), his servants conspired together against him for the blood of the sons of Jehoiada the priest. And they killed him on his bed, and he died. And they buried him in the City of David, but they did not bury him in the graves of the kings.
\v 26 And these {were} the ones having conspired together against him: Zabad, the son of Shimeath the Ammonitess, and Jehozabad, the son of Shimrith the Moabitess.
\v 27 And his sons and the abundance of the burdens against him and the founding of the house of God, behold them, written in the treatise of the scroll of the kings. And Amaziah his son reigned in his place.
\c 25
\p
\v 1 Amaziah {was} a son of 25 years {when} he reigned, and he reigned 29 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Jehoaddan, from Jerusalem.
\v 2 And he did right in the eyes of Yahweh, only not with a whole heart.
\v 3 And it happened, when the kingdom was strong to him, that he struck his servants, the ones having struck the king, his father.
\v 4 But he did not kill their sons, for as written in the law, in the scroll of Moses, what Yahweh commanded, saying, “Fathers shall not be killed for sons, and sons shall not be killed for fathers. Rather, a man shall be killed for his sin.”
\p
\v 5 And Amaziah gathered Judah, and he appointed them according to the house of the fathers, for commanders of thousands and for commanders of hundreds for all of Judah and Benjamin. And he mustered them from a son of 20 years and upward. And he found them, 300,000 chosen, going out to war grasping spear and large shield.
\v 6 And he hired from Israel 100,000 warriors of valor for 100 kikkars of silver.
\v 7 But a man of God came to him, saying, “O King, let not the army of Israel come with you, for Yahweh is not with Israel, all of the sons of Ephraim.
\v 8 But you, come, act, be strong in battle. God will make you stumble to the face of the enemy, for there is power in God to help and to make stumble.”
\v 9 And Amaziah said to the man of God, “And what to do about the 100 kikkars that I have given to the troop of Israel?” And the man of God said, “There is to Yahweh to give to you to multiply from this.”
\v 10 And Amaziah separated them, the troop that had come to him from Ephraim, to go to their place. And their nose burned greatly against Judah, and they returned to their place in burning of nose.
\v 11 And Amaziah strengthened himself, and he led forth his people. And he went to the Valley of Salt, and he struck the sons of Seir, 10,000.
\v 12 And the sons of Judah captured 10,000 alive. And they brought them to the head of a cliff, and they threw them from the head of the cliff. And all of them were split open.
\v 13 But the sons of the troop that Amaziah had sent back from going with him to battle, they raided against the cities of Judah from Samaria and to Beth Horon. And they struck 3,000 from them, and they plundered much plunder.
\p
\v 14 And it happened after Amaziah came from striking the Edomites that he brought the gods of the sons of Seir, and set them up for himself as gods. And he prostrated himself to the face of them, and he sacrificed to them.
\v 15 And the nose of Yahweh burned against Amaziah. And he sent a prophet to him, and he said to him, “Why do you seek the gods of a people that did not deliver their people from your hand?”
\v 16 And it happened, when he was speaking with him, that he said to him, “Have we made you a counselor to the king? Stop yourself! Why should they strike you?” And the prophet stopped. But he said, “I know that God has counseled to destroy you, because you have done this and you have not listened to my counsel.”
\p
\v 17 And Amaziah, the king of Judah, took counsel, and he sent to Joash, the son of Jehoahaz, the son of Jehu, the king of Israel, saying, “Come, let us see one another’s faces.”
\v 18 And Joash, the king of Israel, sent to Amaziah, the king of Judah, saying, “A thornbush that was in Lebanon sent to a cedar that was in Lebanon, saying, ‘Give your daughter to my son as a wife.’ But a beast of the field that was in Lebanon passed by, and it trampled the thornbush.
\v 19 You have said, ‘Behold, you have struck Edom!’ And your heart has lifted you up to boast. Now stay in your house. Why should you contend with calamity, so that you will fall, you and Judah with you?”
\p
\v 20 But Amaziah did not listen, because it {was} from God, in order to give them into the hand because they sought the gods of Edom.
\v 21 And Joash, the king of Israel, went up, and they saw one another’s faces, he and Amaziah, the king of Judah, at Beth Shemesh, which {is} to Judah.
\v 22 And Judah was struck to the face of Israel. And they fled, a man to his tent.
\v 23 And Joash, the king of Israel, captured Amaziah, the king of Judah, the son of Joash, the son of Ahaziah, at Beth Shemesh. And he brought him {to} Jerusalem, and he broke down the wall of Jerusalem from the Gate of Ephraim to the Gate of the Corner, 400 cubits.
\v 24 And all of the gold and the silver and all of the vessels found in the house of God with Obed Edom and the treasuries of the house of the king and sons of pledges. And he returned {to} Samaria.
\p
\v 25 And Amaziah, the son of Joash, the king of Judah, lived after the death of Joash, the son of Jehoahaz, the king of Israel, 15 years.
\v 26 Now the rest of the matters of Amaziah, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, do not behold them, written on the scroll of the kings of Judah and Israel?
\v 27 And from the time that Amaziah turned from after Yahweh, they conspired against him a conspiracy in Jerusalem. And he fled to Lachish. But they sent after him to Lachish, and they killed him there.
\v 28 And they carried him on the horses, and they buried him with his fathers in the city of Judah.
\c 26
\p
\v 1 And all of the people of Judah took Uzziah (now he {was} a son of 16 years), and they made him reign in place of his father Amaziah.
\v 2 He, he built up Eloth, and he returned it to Judah after the king lay down with his fathers.
\v 3 Uzziah {was} a son of 16 years when he reigned, and he reigned 52 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Jekoliah, from Jerusalem.
\v 4 And he did right in the eyes of Yahweh, like all that Amaziah his father had done.
\v 5 And he was seeking God in the days of Zechariah, the one teaching in the seeing of God. And in the days of his seeking Yahweh, God made him prosper.
\p
\v 6 And he went out and fought with the Philistines, and he broke through the wall of Gath and the wall of Jabneh and the wall of Ashdod. And he built cities in Ashdod and among the Philistines.
\v 7 And God helped him against the Philistines and against the Arabians dwelling in Gur Baal and the Meunites.
\v 8 And the Ammonites gave tribute to Uzziah. And his name went to the entrance of Egypt because he was being strong to upward.
\v 9 And Uzziah built towers in Jerusalem at the Gate of the Corner and at the Gate of the Valley and at the Angle, and he strengthened them.
\v 10 And he built towers in the wilderness, and he dug out many cisterns, for much cattle was to him, both in the Shephelah and in the plain. Farmers and vinedressers in the hills and in Carmel, for he was a lover of the ground.
\v 11 And an army of doers of battle was to Uzziah, goers out for war according to the troop by the number of their mustering by the hand of Jeiel, the scribe, and Maaseiah, the officer, at the hand of Hananiah from the commanders of the king.
\v 12 The whole number of the heads of fathers of warriors of valor {was} 2,600.
\v 13 And at their hand {was} an army of war, 307,500 doers of battle, with strength of power to help the king against the enemy.
\v 14 And Uzziah prepared for them, for all of the army, shields and spears and helmets and armor and bows and stones of slings.
\v 15 And he made war machines in Jerusalem, the invention of an inventor, to be on the towers and on the corners to shoot arrows and great stones. And his name went out to far away, for he did wondrously for {him} to be helped until that he was strong.
\p
\v 16 But according to his strength, his heart became high to do corruptly, and he transgressed against Yahweh, his God, and he went into the temple of Yahweh to burn incense on the altar of incense.
\v 17 And Azariah the priest went in after him, and with him the priests of Yahweh, 80 sons of valor.
\v 18 And they stood against Uzziah the king, and they said to him, “{It is} not for you, Uzziah, to burn incense to Yahweh, but for the priests, the sons of Aaron, the ones consecrated to burn incense. Go out from the sanctuary, for you have transgressed, and {it will} not {be} for honor for you from Yahweh God.”
\v 19 And Uzziah raged, and in his hand {was} a censer to burn incense. And when he raged with the priests, then leprosy rose on his forehead to the face of the priests in the house of Yahweh from beside the altar of incense.
\v 20 And Azariah the head priest turned to him, and all of the priests, and behold him, stricken with leprosy on his forehead. And they hastened him from there, and also he, he hastened himself to go out, for Yahweh had struck him.
\v 21 And Uzziah the king was stricken with leprosy until the day of his death. And he dwelled in a house of separation, being stricken with leprosy, for he was cut off from the house of Yahweh. And Jotham his son {was} over the house of the king, judging the people of the land.
\v 22 Now the rest of the matters of Uzziah, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, Isaiah, the son of Amoz, the prophet, wrote.
\v 23 And Uzziah lay down with his fathers. And they buried him with his fathers in the field of the grave that {was} to the kings, for they said, “He was stricken with leprosy.” And Jotham his son reigned in his place.
\c 27
\p
\v 1 Jotham {was} a son of 25 years when he reigned, and he reigned 16 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Jerushah, the daughter of Zadok.
\v 2 And he did right in the eyes of Yahweh like all that Uzziah his father had done, except he did not go into the temple of Yahweh. But the people were still acting corruptly.
\v 3 He, he built the upper gate of the house of Yahweh, and on the wall of Ophel he built in abundance.
\v 4 And he built cities in the hill country of Judah, and in the forests he built fortresses and towers.
\v 5 And he, he fought with the king of the sons of Ammon, and he was strong over them. And the sons of Ammon gave to him in that year 100 kikkars of silver and 10,000 kors {of} wheat and 10,000 {of} barley. The sons of Ammon brought this to him again both in the second year and the third.
\v 6 And Jotham strengthened himself, for he established his ways to the face of Yahweh his God.
\v 7 Now the rest of the matters of Jotham and all of his battles and his ways, behold them, written on the scroll of the kings of Israel and Judah.
\v 8 He was a son of 25 years when he reigned, and he reigned 16 years in Jerusalem.
\v 9 And Jotham lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in the City of David. And Ahaz his son reigned in his place.
\c 28
\p
\v 1 Ahaz {was} a son of 20 years when he reigned, and he reigned 16 years in Jerusalem. And he did not do right in the eyes of Yahweh like David his father,
\v 2 but he walked in the ways of the kings of Israel and he also made molded images for the Baals.
\v 3 And he, he burned incense in the Valley of Ben Hinnom. And he made his sons pass through the fire, like the abominations of the nations that Yahweh had driven out from the face of the sons of Israel.
\v 4 And he sacrificed and burned incense at the high places and on hills and under every green tree.
\p
\v 5 And Yahweh his God gave him into the hand of the king of Aram. And he struck him, and they captured a great captivity from him, and they brought {them} {to} Damascus. And he also gave {him} into the hand of the king of Israel, and he struck him a great striking.
\v 6 And Pekah, the son of Remaliah, killed 120,000 in Judah in one day, all sons of valor, because they had abandoned Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\v 7 And Zichri, a warrior of Ephraim, killed Maaseiah, the son of the king, and Azrikam, the leader of the house, and Elkanah, the second of the king.
\v 8 And the sons of Israel captured from their brothers 200,000 wives, sons, and daughters. And they also plundered much spoil from them. And they brought the spoil back to Samaria.
\v 9 And a prophet of Yahweh was there, Oded {was} his name. And he went out to the face of the army coming to Samaria, and he said to them, “Behold, Yahweh, the God of your fathers, {was} in wrath against Judah. He gave them into your hand. But you killed them in rage. It has reached to the heavens.
\v 10 And now you are saying to subdue the sons of Judah and Jerusalem as male slaves and as female slaves for yourselves. But you, {are} not guilts with you against Yahweh your God?
\v 11 And now, hear me and bring back the captivity that you have captured from your brothers, for the burning of the nose of Yahweh {is} upon you.”
\v 12 And men arose from the heads of the sons of Ephraim, Azariah the son of Jehohanan, Berechiah the son of Meshillemoth, and Jehizkiah the son of Shallum and Amasa the son of Hadlai, against the ones coming from the army.
\v 13 And they said to them, “Do not bring the captivity here, for you are saying to add guilt of Yahweh against us to our sins and to our guilts. For the guilt to us and the burning of the nose against Israel {is} great.”
\v 14 And the armed one forsook the captivity and the plunder to the face of the princes and all of the assembly.
\v 15 And men who were designated by names arose, and they grasped the captivity, and they clothed all of their naked ones from the spoil. And they clothed them, and they sandaled them, and they fed them, and they gave them drink. And they anointed them, and they led them, all of the stumbling ones, on donkeys. And they brought them {to} Jericho, the City of Palms, near their brothers. Then they returned {to} Samaria.
\p
\v 16 At that time, King Ahaz sent to the kings of Assyria to help him.
\v 17 And again the Edomites came, and they struck Judah, and they captured a captivity.
\v 18 And the Philistines raided among the cities of the Shepelah and the Negev of Judah. And they captured Beth Shemesh and Aijalon and Gederoth and Soko and its daughters, and Timnah and its daughters, and Gimzo and its daughters. And they dwelled there.
\v 19 For Yahweh humbled Judah because of Ahaz, the king of Israel, for he broke loose in Judah and transgressed transgression against Yahweh.
\v 20 And Tilgath Pilneser, the king of Assyria, came to him. But he narrowed to him, and he did not strengthen him.
\v 21 For Ahaz divided the house of Yahweh and the house of the king and the princes, and he gave to the king of Assyria, but {this was} not a help to him.
\p
\v 22 And in the time of narrowness to him, then he added to transgress against Yahweh, he, King Ahaz.
\v 23 And he sacrificed to the gods of Damascus, the ones striking him, and he said, “Because the gods of the kings of Aram, they are helping them, to them I will sacrifice, and they will help me.” But they, they were to him and to all of Israel a causing to stumble.
\v 24 And Ahaz assembled the vessels of the house of God, and he cut up the vessels of the house of God, and he shut the doors of the house of Yahweh. And he made altars for himself on every corner in Jerusalem.
\v 25 And in every city and city of Judah, he made high places to sacrifice to other gods. And he provoked Yahweh, the God of his fathers.
\v 26 Now the rest of his matters and all of his ways, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, behold them, written on the scroll of the kings of Judah and Israel.
\v 27 And Ahaz lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in the city, in Jerusalem, but they did not bring him to the graves of the kings of Israel. And Hezekiah his son reigned in his place.
\c 29
\p
\v 1 Hezekiah reigned {as} a son of 25 years, and he reigned 29 years in Jerusalem. And the name of his mother {was} Abijah, the daughter of Zechariah.
\v 2 And he did right in the eyes of Yahweh, like all that David his father did.
\v 3 He, in the first year of his reigning, in the first month, he opened the doors of the house of Yahweh, and he strengthened them.
\v 4 And he brought in the priests and the Levites, and he assembled them in the open area {to} the east.
\v 5 And he said to them, “Hear me, Levites! Now consecrate yourselves and consecrate the house of Yahweh, the God of your fathers, and bring the impurity out from the holy place.
\v 6 For our fathers have transgressed, and they have done evil in the eyes of Yahweh our God. And they have abandoned him, and they have turned their faces from the tabernacle of Yahweh, and they have turned the back.
\v 7 They have also shut the doors of the porch, and they have extinguished the lamps, and they have not offered incense, and they have not offered burnt offerings in the holy place of the God of Israel.
\v 8 And the wrath of Yahweh has been on Judah and Jerusalem. And he has made them a terror, a horror, and a hissing, just as you are seeing with your eyes.
\v 9 And behold, our fathers have fallen by the sword, and our sons and our daughters and our wives {are} in captivity because of this.
\v 10 Now {it is} with my heart to cut a covenant with Yahweh, the God of Israel, that the burning of his nose may turn from us.
\v 11 My sons, now do not be negligent, for Yahweh has chosen you to stand to his face to minister {to} him and to be ministering to him and offering sacrifices.”
\p
\v 12 And the Levites arose, Mahath, the son of Amasai, and Joel, the son of Azariah, from the sons of the Kohathites, and from the sons of Merari, Kish, the son of Abdi, and Azariah, the son of Jehallelel, and from the Gershonites, Joah, the son of Zimmah, and Eden, the son of Joah,
\v 13 and from the sons of Elizaphan, Shimri and Jeuel, and from the sons of Asaph, Zechariah and Mattaniah,
\v 14 and from the sons of Heman, Jehuel and Shimei; and from the sons of Jeduthun, Shemaiah and Uzziel.
\v 15 And they assembled their brothers, and they consecrated themselves, and they entered according to the commandment of the king by the words of Yahweh to cleanse the house of Yahweh.
\v 16 And the priests went into the inside of the house of Yahweh to cleanse, and they brought out all of the uncleanness that they found in the temple of Yahweh to the courtyard of the house of Yahweh. And the Levites took {it} to bring {it} out the wadi of Kidron, outside.
\v 17 And they began to consecrate on the first of the first month. And on the day of eight to the month, they came to the porch of Yahweh, and they consecrated the house of Yahweh for eight days. And on the day of sixteen to the first month, they finished.
\v 18 And they came inside to King Hezekiah, and they said, “We have cleansed all of the house of Yahweh, the altar of burnt offerings and all of its vessels and the table of the arrangement and all of its vessels.
\v 19 And all of the vessels that King Ahaz rejected in his reign in his transgression, we have prepared and we have consecrated, and behold them, to the face of the altar of Yahweh.”
\p
\v 20 And King Hezekiah arose early and assembled the princes of the city. And he went up {to} the house of Yahweh.
\v 21 And they brought seven bulls and seven rams and seven lambs and seven male goats of goats for a sin offering for the kingdom and for the sanctuary and for Judah. And he said to the sons of Aaron, the priests, to make {them} go up on the altar of Yahweh.
\v 22 And they slaughtered the cattle, and the priests took the blood, and they sprinkled {it} on the altar. And they slaughtered the rams, and they sprinkled the blood on the altar. And they slaughtered the lambs, and they sprinkled the blood on the altar.
\v 23 And they brought near the goats of the sin offering to the face of the king and the assembly, and they laid their hands on them.
\v 24 And the priests slaughtered them, and they made their blood a sin offering on the altar to cover over all of Israel, because “for all of Israel,” the king said, “{are} the burnt offering and the sin offering.”
\p
\v 25 And he stationed the Levites {in} the house of Yahweh with cymbals, with lyres, and with harps, according to the commandment of David and Gad, the seer of the king, and Nathan the prophet, for by the hand of Yahweh {was} the commandment by the hand of his prophets.
\v 26 And the Levites stood with the instruments of David, and the priests, with the trumpets.
\v 27 And Hezekiah said to offer the burnt offering on the altar. And at the time the burnt offering began, the song of Yahweh began, and the trumpets, and under the hands of the instruments of David, the king of Israel.
\v 28 And all of the assembly was prostrating themselves, and the song was singing, and the trumpets were sounding, all until to the finishing of the burnt offering.
\p
\v 29 And according to the finishing of the burnt offering, the king and all of the ones found with him bowed down, and they prostrated themselves.
\v 30 And King Hezekiah and the princes said to the Levites to praise Yahweh according to the words of David and Asaph the seer. And they praised unto rejoicing, and they bowed down and they prostrated themselves.
\p
\v 31 And Hezekiah answered and said, “You, you have filled your hand to Yahweh. Come near and bring in sacrifices and thank offerings to the house of Yahweh.” And the assembly brought in sacrifices and thank offerings, and all of the willing of heart, burnt offerings.
\v 32 And the number of the burnt offering that the assembly brought in was 70 bulls, 100 rams, 200 lambs, all of these for the burnt offering to Yahweh.
\v 33 And the consecrated {were} 600 bulls and 3,000 sheep.
\v 34 But the priests were {too} few, and they were not able to skin all of the burnt offerings, so their brothers, the Levites, strengthened them until the finishing of the work and until the priests had consecrated themselves, for the Levites were right of heart to consecrate themselves more than the priests,
\v 35 and also the burnt offering {was} in abundance with the fat of the peace offerings and with the drink offerings for the burnt offering. And the service of the house of Yahweh was established.
\v 36 And Hezekiah and all of the people rejoiced over what God had prepared for the people, for the matter had happened in suddenness.
\c 30
\p
\v 1 And Hezekiah sent to all of Israel and Judah, and he also wrote letters to Ephraim and Manasseh, to come to the house of Yahweh in Jerusalem to make a Passover to Yahweh, the God of Israel.
\v 2 And the king and his princes and all of the assembly in Jerusalem counseled to make the Passover in the second month,
\v 3 for they were not able to make it at that time because the priests had not consecrated themselves for sufficiency and the people had not assembled to Jerusalem.
\v 4 And the matter was right in the eyes of the king and in the eyes of all of the assembly.
\v 5 And they made a word stand to make a voice pass through among all of Israel from Beersheba and to Dan to come to make a Passover to Yahweh, the God of Israel, in Jerusalem, for they had not done {this} to abundance as was written.
\v 6 And runners went with letters from the hand of the king and his princes among all of Israel and Judah and according to the command of the king, saying, “Sons of Israel, return to Yahweh, the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Israel, and he will return to the escape remaining to you from the palm of the kings of Assyria.
\v 7 And may you not be like your fathers and your brothers, who transgressed against Yahweh, the God of their fathers. And he made them a horror, just as you are seeing.
\v 8 Now, may you not harden your neck like your fathers. Give a hand to Yahweh, and come to his sanctuary, which he has consecrated to eternity, and serve Yahweh your God, that he may turn the burning of his nose from you.
\v 9 For when you return to Yahweh, your brothers and your sons {will be} for mercies to the face of their captors, and they will return to this land. For Yahweh your God {is} gracious and compassionate, and he will not turn the face from you, if you return to him.”
\p
\v 10 And the runners were passing through from city to city in the land of Ephraim and Manasseh and unto Zebulun, but they were laughing at them and mocking them.
\v 11 However, men from Asher and Manasseh and from Zebulun humbled themselves and came to Jerusalem.
\v 12 Also the hand of God was on Judah to give one heart to them to do the command of the king and the princes by the word of Yahweh.
\p
\v 13 And many people assembled {in} Jerusalem to make the Feast of Unleavened Bread in the second month, an assembly to great abundance.
\v 14 And they arose and removed the altars that {were} in Jerusalem, and they removed all of the incense altars, and they threw {them} into the wadi of Kidron.
\v 15 And they slaughtered the Passover on 14 to the second month. And the priests and the Levites were ashamed, and they consecrated themselves, and they brought burnt offerings to the house of Yahweh.
\v 16 And they stood at their stations according to their regulation, according to the law of Moses, the man of God. The priests were sprinkling the blood from the hand of the Levites,
\v 17 for many {were} in the assembly who had not consecrated themselves. So the Levites {were} over the slaughtering of the Passovers for all {who were} not clean to consecrate {it} to Yahweh.
\v 18 For a multitude of people, many from Ephraim and Manasseh, Issachar and Zebulun, had not purified themselves, but they ate the Passover, not according to {what} is written. But Hezekiah prayed for them, saying, “May good Yahweh cover over
\v 19 everyone {who} has prepared his heart to seek God, Yahweh, the God of his fathers, but not according to the purifying of the sanctuary.”
\v 20 And Yahweh listened to Hezekiah, and he healed the people.
\v 21 And the sons of Israel found in Jerusalem made the Feast of Unleavened Bread seven days with great rejoicing. And the Levites and the priests were praising Yahweh day by day with instruments of might to Yahweh.
\v 22 And Hezekiah spoke to the heart of all of the Levites having insight {with} good insight of Yahweh. And they ate the appointed feast seven days, sacrificing sacrifices of peace offerings and thanking Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\p
\v 23 And all of the assembly consulted to make seven days after them, and they made seven days {with} rejoicing.
\v 24 For Hezekiah, the king of Judah, brought up 1,000 bulls and 7,000 sheep to the assembly, and the princes brought up 1,000 bulls and 10,000 sheep to the assembly. And many priests consecrated themselves.
\v 25 And all of the assembly of Judah and the priests and the Levites and all of the assembly having come from Israel and the sojourners having come from the land of Israel and the dwellers of Judah rejoiced.
\v 26 And great rejoicing was in Jerusalem, for from the days of Solomon, the son of David, the king of Israel, like this {had} not {been} in Jerusalem.
\v 27 And the priests, the Levites, arose, and they blessed the people. And their voice was heard, and their prayer came to the dwelling of his holiness in the heavens.
\c 31
\p
\v 1 And with the finishing of all of this, all of Israel being found went out to the cities of Judah, and they shattered the pillars. And they cut down the Asherahs, and they tore down the high places and the altars from all of Judah and Benjamin and in Ephraim and Manasseh, until finishing. And all of the sons of Israel returned, a man to his possession, to their cities.
\p
\v 2 And Hezekiah stationed the divisions of the priests and the Levites according to their divisions, a man according to the mouth of his service, for the priests and for the Levites for burnt offering and for peace offerings, to minister and to give thanks and to praise at the gates of the camp of Yahweh.
\v 3 And the portion of the king {was} from his property for the burnt offerings, for the burnt offerings of the morning and the evening and the burnt offerings for the Sabbaths and for the new moons and for the annual festivals, as written in the law of Yahweh.
\v 4 And he said to the people, to the dwellers of Jerusalem to give the portion of the priests and the Levites, so that they might be strong in the law of Yahweh.
\v 5 And when the word broke out, the sons of Israel made abundant firstfruits of grain, new wine, and oil, and honey and all of the produce of the field. And they brought in the tithe of all of the abundance.
\v 6 And the sons of Israel and Judah, the dwellers in the cities of Judah, they also, they brought in the tithe of cattle and sheep and the tithe of the holy things consecrated to Yahweh their God. And they gave heaps, heaps.
\v 7 In the third month, they began to found the heaps, and in the seventh month, they finished.
\v 8 And Hezekiah and the princes came, and they saw the heaps. And they blessed Yahweh and his people Israel.
\v 9 And Hezekiah sought after the priests and the Levites about the heaps.
\v 10 And Azariah, the head priest of the house of Zadok, spoke to him, and he said, “From the beginning of the bringing of the contribution to the house of Yahweh, we have eaten and been full and left over in abundance, for Yahweh has blessed his people, and this abundance has been left.”
\p
\v 11 And Hezekiah said to prepare chambers in the house of Yahweh, and they prepared {them}.
\v 12 And they brought in the contribution and the tithe and the holy things in faithfulness. And Conaniah the Levite {was} over them as a leader, and Shimei his brother {was} second.
\v 13 And Jehiel and Azaziah and Nahath and Asahel and Jerimoth and Jozabad and Eliel and Ismakiah and Mahath and Benaiah {were} overseers at the hand of Conaniah and Shimei his brother, by the appointment of King Hezekiah and Azariah, the leader of the house of God.
\v 14 And Kore, the son of Imnah, the Levite, the gatekeeper to the east, {was} over the free will offerings of God, to give the contribution of Yahweh and the holy things of holy things,
\v 15 and at his hand {were} Eden and Miniamin and Jeshua and Shemaiah and Amariah and Shecaniah in the cities of the priests, to give in faithfulness to their brothers in divisions, like great, like small
\v 16 (apart from the ones being enrolled by genealogy, to males from a son of three years and upward, to all coming to the house of Yahweh for the matter of a day in its day for their service, by their keepings according to their divisions
\v 17 —now the enrolling by genealogy of the priests {was} by the house of their fathers)—and the Levites from a son of 20 years and upward, by their keepings according to their divisions),
\v 18 and to the enrolling by genealogy of all of their family, their wives and their sons and their daughters, to all of the assembly, for in their faithfulness they were consecrating themselves in holiness.
\v 19 And for the sons of Aaron, the priests, in the fields of open land of their cities, in each city and city, {were} men who were designated by names to give portions to every male among the priests and to all being enrolled by genealogy among the Levites.
\p
\v 20 And Hezekiah did like this in all of Judah. And he did good and right and faithful to the face of Yahweh his God.
\v 21 And in every deed that he began in the service of the house of God and in the law and in the commandment to seek his God, he did with all of his heart, and he prospered.
\c 32
\p
\v 1 After these things and faithfulness, Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, came. And he came into Judah. And he encamped against the cities of fortification, and he said to capture them for himself.
\v 2 And Hezekiah saw that Sennacherib had come and his face {was} to battle against Jerusalem.
\v 3 And he consulted with his commanders and his warriors to stop up the waters of the springs that {were} from outside to the city, and they helped him.
\v 4 And many people gathered, and they stopped up all of the springs and the stream flowing in the midst of the land, saying, “Why should the kings of Assyria come and find many waters?”
\v 5 And he strengthened himself. And he built up all of the broken wall, and he brought up onto the towers, and to the outside {of} the wall, another. And he strengthened the Millo {in} the city of David. And he made weapons in abundance, and shields.
\v 6 And he put commanders of battle over the people. And he gathered them to himself at the open area of the gate of the city. And he spoke to their heart, saying,
\v 7 “Be strong and be courageous. May you not fear, and may you not be dismayed from the face of the king of Assyria and from the face of all of the multitude that {is} with him, for with us {is} {one} greater than with him.
\v 8 With him {is} an arm of flesh, but with us {is} Yahweh our God to help us and to fight our battles.” And the people supported themselves on the words of Hezekiah, the king of Judah.
\p
\v 9 After this, Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, sent his servants {to} Jerusalem (now he {was} at Lachish, and all of his dominion {was} with him) to Hezekiah, the king of Judah, and to all of Judah that {was} in Jerusalem, saying,
\v 10 “Thus says Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, ‘On what are you trusting and dwelling in siege in Jerusalem?
\v 11 Is not Hezekiah misleading you, to give you to die by famine and by thirst, saying, “Yahweh our God will deliver us from the palm of the king of Assyria’”?
\v 12 Has not he, Hezekiah, removed his high places and his altars and said to Judah and to Jerusalem, saying, “To the face of one altar you shall prostrate yourselves, and on it you shall sacrifice”?
\v 13 Do you not know what I have done, I and my fathers, to all of the peoples of the lands? Being able, were the gods of the nations of the lands able to deliver their land from my hand?
\v 14 Who {is it} among all of the gods of these nations that my fathers have devoted to destruction that has been able to deliver its people from my hand, that your God should be able to deliver you from my hand?
\v 15 And now, let not Hezekiah deceive you, and let him not mislead you like this. And may you not believe him, for any god {of} any nation or kingdom has not been able to deliver its people from my hand or from the hand of my fathers. How much more will your God not deliver you from my hand!’”
\v 16 And his servants spoke further against Yahweh God and against Hezekiah his servant.
\v 17 And he wrote letters to taunt Yahweh, the God of Israel, and to speak against him, saying, “Like the gods of the nations of the lands that did not deliver their people from my hand, thus the God of Hezekiah will not deliver his people from my hand.”
\v 18 And they called in a great voice {in} Judean to the people of Jerusalem who {were} on the wall to frighten them and to terrify them so that they might capture the city.
\v 19 And they spoke about the God of Jerusalem as against the gods of the peoples of the land, the work of the hands of man.
\p
\v 20 And King Hezekiah and Isaiah, the son of Amoz, the prophet. prayed about this, and they cried out {to} the heavens.
\v 21 And Yahweh sent an angel, and he annihilated every warrior of the army and leader and commander in the camp of the king of Assyria. And he returned with shame of face to his land. And he entered the house of his god, and some of the ones coming forth from his insides made him fall by the sword there.
\v 22 And Yahweh saved Hezekiah and the dwellers of Jerusalem from the hand of Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, and from the hand of all. And he gave them rest from all around.
\v 23 And many were bringing a gift to Yahweh to Jerusalem and valuables to Hezekiah, the king of Judah. And he was lifted up in the eyes of all of the nations from after that.
\p
\v 24 In those days Hezekiah was sick unto death. And he prayed to Yahweh, and he spoke to him, and he gave a sign to him.
\v 25 But Hezekiah did not return according to the benefit to him, for his heart was high. And wrath was on him and on Judah and Jerusalem.
\v 26 But Hezekiah humbled himself from the height of his heart, he and the dwellers of Jerusalem, and the wrath of Yahweh did not come upon them in the days of Hezekiah.
\p
\v 27 And {there} was to Hezekiah very much riches and glory. And he made treasuries for himself for silver and for gold and for precious stone and for spices and for shields and for every vessel of delight
\v 28 and storehouses for produce of grain and new wine and oil and stalls for every beast and beast and flocks for stalls.
\v 29 And he made cities for himself and livestock of sheep and cattle to abundance, for God gave very much property to him.
\v 30 And he, Hezekiah, stopped up the upper outlet of the waters of Gihon, and he directed them down to the west of the City of David. And Hezekiah prospered in all of his work.
\v 31 And thus, when the princes of Babylon dispatched envoys to him to seek the sign that was in the land, God left him to test him, to know everything in his heart.
\p
\v 32 Now the rest of the matters of Hezekiah and his covenant faithfulnesses, behold them, written in the vision of Isaiah, the son of Amoz, the prophet, on the scroll of the kings of Judah and Israel.
\v 33 And Hezekiah lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in the ascent of the graves of the sons of David. And all of Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem did honor for him in his death. And Manasseh his son reigned in his place.
\c 33
\p
\v 1 Manasseh {was} a son of 12 years when he reigned, and he reigned 55 years in Jerusalem.
\v 2 And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh, like the abominations of the nations that Yahweh had driven out from the face of the sons of Israel.
\v 3 And he returned and built the high places that Hezekiah his father had destroyed. And he set up altars for the Baals, and he made Asherahs, and he prostrated himself to all of the army of the heavens, and he served them.
\v 4 And he built altars in the house of Yahweh, {about} which Yahweh had said, “My name will be in Jerusalem to eternity.”
\v 5 And he built altars for all of the army of the heavens in the two of the courtyards of the house of Yahweh.
\v 6 And he, he made his sons pass through the fire in the Valley of Ben Hinnom, and he practiced divination, and he observed signs, and he used witchcraft, and he made a ritual pit and familiar spirits. He multiplied doing evil in the eyes of Yahweh, provoking him.
\v 7 And he put the image of the idol that he made in the house of God, {about} which God had said to David and to Solomon his son, “In this house and in Jerusalem, which I have chosen from all of the tribes of Israel, I will put my name to eternity,
\v 8 and I will not add to make the foot of Israel wander from upon the ground that I appointed to your fathers if only they will take care to do all that I have commanded them and all of the law and the statutes and the judgments by the hand of Moses.”
\v 9 But Manasseh misled Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem to do more evil than the nations that Yahweh had destroyed from the face of the sons of Israel.
\p
\v 10 And Yahweh spoke to Manasseh and to his people, but they did not attend.
\v 11 And Yahweh brought against them the commanders of the army that {was} to the king of Assyria. And they captured Manasseh with hooks, and they bound him in bronze, and they made him go into Babylon.
\v 12 But when distress was to him, he entreated the face of Yahweh, his God, and he humbled himself greatly from to the face of the God of his fathers.
\v 13 And he prayed to him; and he was entreated by him, and he heard his supplication, and he brought him back {to} Jerusalem, to his kingdom. And Manasseh knew that Yahweh, he {is} God.
\p
\v 14 And after this, he built an outer wall for the City of David, from the west to Gihon, in the wadi, and at the entrance of the Gate of Fish and around to Ophel, and he made it very high. And he put commanders of the army in all of the cities of fortification in Judah.
\v 15 And he removed the gods of the foreigner and the idol from the house of Yahweh and all of the altars that he had built on the mountain of the house of Yahweh and in Jerusalem. And he threw {them} outside of the city.
\v 16 And he established the altar of Yahweh, and he sacrificed on it sacrifices of peace offerings and a thank offering. And he said to Judah to serve Yahweh, the God of Israel.
\v 17 Nevertheless, the people were still sacrificing on the high places, only to Yahweh their God.
\p
\v 18 Now the rest of the matters of Manasseh and his prayer to his God and the words of the seers, the ones speaking to him in the name of Yahweh, the God of Israel, behold them, on the matters of the kings of Israel.
\v 19 And his prayer and his being entreated by him and all of his sin and his transgression and the places where he built high places on them and stationed Asherahs and idols to the face of his humbling himself, behold them, written in the words of Hozai.
\v 20 And Manasseh lay down with his fathers, and they buried him in his house. And Amon his son reigned in his place.
\p
\v 21 Amon was a son of 22 years when he reigned, and he reigned two years in Jerusalem.
\v 22 And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh, just as Manasseh his father had done. And to all of the idols that Manasseh his father had made, Amon sacrificed, and he served them.
\v 23 And he did not humble himself from to the face of Yahweh, as Manasseh his father had humbled himself, but he, Amon, he multiplied guilt.
\v 24 And his servants conspired against him, and they killed him in his house.
\v 25 But the people of the land struck all of the ones having conspired against King Amon, and the people of the land made Josiah his son reign in his place.
\c 34
\p
\v 1 Josiah was a son of 8 years when he reigned, and he reigned 31 years in Jerusalem.
\v 2 And he did right in the eyes of Yahweh, and he walked in the ways of David his father, and he did not turn right or left.
\v 3 And in eight years to his reigning, and he, he {was} still a youth, he began to seek the God of David, his father. And in year twelve, he began to cleanse Judah and Jerusalem from the high places and the Asherahs and the idols and the molded images.
\v 4 And they tore down to his face the altars of the Baals, and the sun images that {were} to above from upon them, he cut down. And he shattered the Asherahs and the idols and the molded images and crushed {them}, and he scattered {the dust} upon the faces of the graves of the ones having sacrificed to them.
\v 5 And he burned the bones of the priests on their altars. And he cleansed Judah and Jerusalem.
\v 6 And in the cities of Manasseh and Ephraim and Simeon and unto Naphtali, in their ruins all around,
\v 7 he also tore down the altars, and he crushed the Asherahs and the idols to grinding, and he cut apart all of the sun images in all of the land of Israel. And he returned to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 8 And in the year of 18 to his reigning, for cleansing the land and the house, he sent Shaphan, the son of Azaliah, and Maaseiah, the prince of the city, and Joah, the son of Joahaz, the recorder, to strengthen the house of Yahweh his God.
\v 9 And they came to Hilkiah, the great priest, and they gave {him} the silver having been brought {to} the house of God that the Levites, the keepers of the threshold, had gathered from the hand of Manasseh and Ephraim and from all of the remnant of Israel and from all of Judah and Benjamin and the dwellers of Jerusalem.
\v 10 And they gave {it} into the hand of the doer of the work, the ones appointed over the house of Yahweh. And they gave it {to} the doers of the work who were working on the house of Yahweh to repair and to strengthen the house.
\v 11 And they gave {it} to the craftsmen and the builders to buy stones of hewing and wood for braces and to beam the houses that the kings of Judah had destroyed.
\v 12 And the men were working in faithfulness in the work. And Jahath and Obadiah, Levites from the sons of Merari, and Zechariah and Meshullam from the sons of the Kohathites were appointed to supervise over them (now the Levites {were} all understanding in instruments of song}
\v 13 and over the bearers and overseers for every doer of the work for service and service. And from the Levites {were} scribes and officials and gatekeepers.
\p
\v 14 And when they were bringing out the silver having been brought {to} the house of Yahweh, Hilkiah the priest found the scroll of the law of Yahweh by the hand of Moses.
\v 15 And Hilkiah answered and said to Shaphan the scribe, “I have found the scroll of the law in the house of Yahweh.” And Hilkiah gave the scroll to Shaphan.
\v 16 And Shaphan brought the scroll to the king, and he returned a further word {to} the king, saying, “All that was put into the hand of your servants, they are doing.
\v 17 And they have poured out the silver found in the house of Yahweh, and they have given it into the hand of the appointed ones and into the hand of the doers of the work.”
\v 18 And Shaphan the scribe declared to the king, saying, “Hilkiah the priest has given a scroll to me.” And Shaphan read from it to the face of the king.
\v 19 And it happened when the king heard the words of the law that he tore his clothes.
\v 20 And the king commanded Hilkiah and Ahikam, the son of Shaphan, and Abdon, the son of Micah, and Shaphan the scribe and Asaiah, the servant of the king, saying,
\v 21 “Go, seek Yahweh for me and for the remnant in Israel and in Judah concerning the words of the scroll that has been found. For great {is} the heat of Yahweh that is poured out on us because our fathers have not kept the word of Yahweh to do like everything written on this scroll.”
\p
\v 22 And Hilkiah and {those} whom the king {had sent} went to Huldah the prophetess, the wife of Shallum, the son of Tokhath, the son of Hasrah, the keeper of the garments. And she was dwelling in Jerusalem, in the Second. And they spoke to her like this.
\v 23 And she said to them, “Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, ‘Say to the man who sent you to me,
\v 24 “Thus says Yahweh, ‘Behold me, bringing disaster upon this place and upon its dwellers, all of the curses written on the scroll that they read to the face of the king of Judah,
\v 25 because they have abandoned me and burned incense to other gods so as to provoke me by all of the works of their hands. And my heat will pour out on this place, and it will not extinguish.’”’
\v 26 But to the king of Judah, the one having sent you to seek Yahweh, thus you shall say to him, ‘Thus says Yahweh, the God of Israel, {about} the words that you heard,
\v 27 “Because your heart was tender and you humbled yourself from the face of God when you heard his words about this place and about its dwellers, and you have humbled yourself to my face, and you have torn your clothes, and you have wept to my face, so also I, I have heard”—the declaration of Yahweh—
\v 28 “behold me, gathering you to your fathers. And you will be gathered to your grave in peace, and your eyes will not see all of the disaster that I am bringing on this place and on its dwellers.”’” And they brought back word to the king.
\p
\v 29 And the king sent, and he assembled all of the elders of Judah and Jerusalem.
\v 30 And the king went up {to} the house of Yahweh, and every man of Judah and the dwellers of Jerusalem and the priests and the Levites and all of the people, from great and to small. And he read in their ears all of the words of the scroll of the covenant having been found in the house of Yahweh.
\v 31 And the king stood in his place, and he cut a covenant to the face of Yahweh to walk after Yahweh and to keep his commandments, and his testimonies and his statutes with all of his heart and with all of his soul, to do the words of the covenant written on this scroll.
\v 32 And he made every one found in Jerusalem and Benjamin stand. And the dwellers of Jerusalem did according to the covenant of God, the God of their fathers.
\v 33 And Josiah removed all of the abominations from all of the lands that {were} to the sons of Israel. And he made every one found in Israel serve, to serve Yahweh, their God. All of his days, they did not turn away from after Yahweh, the God of their fathers.
\c 35
\p
\v 1 And Josiah made a Passover in Jerusalem to Yahweh. And they slaughtered the Passover on 14 to the first month.
\v 2 And he stationed the priests over their keepings, and he strengthened them for the service of the house of Yahweh.
\v 3 And he said to the Levites teaching all of Israel, {ones} holy to Yahweh, “Put the Holy Box in the house that Solomon, the son of David, the king of Israel, built. {May it} not {be} to you a burden on your shoulder. Now serve Yahweh your God and his people Israel,
\v 4 and prepare yourselves, by the house of your fathers, according to your divisions, by the writing of David, the king of Israel, and by the writing of Solomon his son,
\v 5 and stand in the holy place according to the divisions of the house of the fathers of your brothers, the sons of the people, and the portion of the house of a father of the Levites,
\v 6 and slaughter the Passover, and consecrate yourselves, and prepare for your brothers, to do according to the word of Yahweh by the hand of Moses.”
\p
\v 7 And Josiah contributed for the sons of the people a flock of lambs and sons of female goats, the whole of the Passovers for all of the ones being found, to the number of 30,000, and 3,000 oxen. These {were} from the property of the king.
\v 8 And his princes contributed to a freewill offering for the people, for the priests, and for the Levites. Hilkiah and Zechariah and Jehiel, leaders of the house of God, gave to the priests for the Passovers 2,600 {sheep} and 300 oxen.
\v 9 And Konaniah and Shemaiah and Nethanel his brothers and Hashabiah and Jeiel and Jozabad, princes of the Levites, contributed for the Levites for the Passovers, 5,000 {sheep} and 300 oxen.
\p
\v 10 And the service was prepared, and the priests stood at their station, and the Levites, by their divisions, according to the commandment of the king.
\v 11 And they slaughtered the Passover, and the priests sprinkled from their hand, and the Levites were skinning.
\v 12 And they removed the burnt offering to give them to the divisions of the house of the fathers for the sons of the people to bring near to Yahweh, as written in the scroll of Moses. And thus for the oxen.
\v 13 And they cooked the Passover in fire according to the regulation, but they cooked the holy {things} in pots and in kettles and in pans, and they ran {them} to all of the sons of the people.
\v 14 And afterward they prepared for themselves and for the priests, because the priests, the sons of Aaron, {were} in the offering of the burnt offering and the fat portions until night, so the Levites prepared for themselves and for the priests, the sons of Aaron.
\v 15 And the singers, the sons of Asaph, {were} at their station, according to the commandment of David and Asaph and Heman and Jeduthun, the seer of the king, and the gatekeepers, for gate and gate. It was not to them to turn aside from at their station, because their brothers, the Levites, prepared for them.
\p
\v 16 And all of the service of Yahweh was prepared on that day to make the Passover and to offer burnt offerings on the altar of Yahweh, according to the commandment of King Josiah.
\v 17 And the sons of Israel being found made the Passover at that time, and the Feast of Unleavened Bread, seven days.
\v 18 Now a Passover like it had not been made in Israel from the days of Samuel the prophet. And all of the kings of Israel had not made like the Passover that Josiah made and the priests and the Levites and all of Judah and Israel being found and the dwellers of Jerusalem.
\v 19 In the year 18 to the reign of Josiah, this Passover was made.
\p
\v 20 After all of this, when Josiah prepared the house, Neco, the king of Egypt, went up to fight at Carchemish by the Euphrates. And Josiah went out to meet him.
\v 21 And he sent messengers to him, saying, “What to me and to you, King of Judah? Not against you, you, today, but against the house of my battle. And God has said for me to hasten. Stop yourself from God, who {is} with me, that he may not destroy you.”
\v 22 But Josiah did not turn his face from him. But to fight with him, he disguised himself. And he did not listen to the words of Neco from the mouth of God. And he came to fight on the plain of Megiddo.
\v 23 And the shooters shot King Josiah, and the king said to his servants, “Take me away, for I have been wounded badly.”
\v 24 And his servants took him away from the chariot, and they made him ride on the second chariot that {was} to him. And they brought him {to} Jerusalem. And he died, and he was buried in the graves of his fathers. And all of Judah and Jerusalem were mourning for Josiah.
\v 25 And Jeremiah lamented for Josiah, and all of the male singers and the female singers have spoken in their laments about Josiah to today. And they made them a statute for Israel, and behold them, written in the laments.
\v 26 Now the rest of the matters of Josiah and his covenant faithfulnesses as written in the law of Yahweh
\v 27 and his matters, the first {ones} and the last {ones}, behold them, written on the scroll of the kings of Israel and Judah.
\c 36
\p
\v 1 And the people of the land took Jehoahaz, the son of Josiah, and they made him reign in Jerusalem in place of his father.
\v 2 Joahaz {was} a son of 23 years when he reigned, and he reigned three months in Jerusalem.
\v 3 And the king of Egypt removed him in Jerusalem, and he fined the land 100 kikkars of silver and a kikkar of gold.
\v 4 And the king of Egypt made Eliakim his brother reign over Judah and Jerusalem, and he changed his name {to} Jehoiakim. But Neco took his brother Joahaz and brought him to Egypt.
\p
\v 5 Jehoiakim {was} a son of 25 years when he reigned, and he reigned 11 years in Jerusalem. And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh his God.
\v 6 Nebuchadnezzar, the king of Babylon, came up against him, and he bound him with {two} bronze {chains} to bring him to Babylon.
\v 7 And Nebuchadnezzar brought some of the vessels of the house of Yahweh to Babylon, and he put them in his temple in Babylon.
\v 8 Now the rest of the matters of Jehoiakim and the abominations that he did and {the thing} found against him, behold them, written on the scroll of the kings of Israel and Judah. And Jehoiachin his son reigned in his place.
\p
\v 9 Jehoiachin {was} a son of eight years when he reigned; and he reigned three months and ten days in Jerusalem. And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh.
\v 10 And at the return of the year, King Nebuchadnezzar sent and brought him to Babylon, with vessels of delight of the house of Yahweh And he made Zedekiah his brother reign over Judah and Jerusalem.
\p
\v 11 Zedekiah {was} a son of 21 years when he reigned, and he reigned 11 years in Jerusalem.
\v 12 And he did evil in the eyes of Yahweh his God. He did not humble himself from the face of Jeremiah the prophet from the mouth of Yahweh.
\v 13 And he also rebelled against King Nebuchadnezzar, who had made him swear by God. And he stiffened his neck and he hardened his heart from turning to Yahweh, the God of Israel.
\v 14 Also all of the princes of the priests and the people made great to transgress transgression like all of the abominations of the nations. And they defiled the house of Yahweh, which he had made holy in Jerusalem.
\v 15 And Yahweh, the God of their fathers, sent to them by the hand of his messengers, rising early and sending, because he had compassion on his people and on his dwelling.
\v 16 But they were mocking the messengers of God and despising his words and mistreating his prophets, until the heat of Yahweh went up against his people, until there was no healing.
\p
\v 17 And he brought up the king of the Chaldeans against them, and he killed their young men with the sword in the house of their sanctuary, and he did not pity a young man or a virgin, old or grey-haired. He gave all into his hand.
\v 18 All of the vessels of the house of God, the great and the small, and the treasures of the house of Yahweh and the treasures of the king and his princes, he brought all {to} Babylon.
\v 19 And they burned the house of God, and they tore down the wall of Jerusalem; and they burned all of its citadels with fire, and they destroyed all of its vessels of delight.
\v 20 And the remnant from the sword, he exiled to Babylon, and they were servants to him and to his sons until the reign of the kingdom of Persia
\v 21 to fulfill the word of Yahweh by the mouth of Jeremiah, until the land had enjoyed its Sabbaths. It rested all of the days of its being desolated to fulfill 70 years.
\p
\v 22 And in the year of one to Cyrus, the king of Persia, to accomplish the word of Yahweh by the mouth of Jeremiah, Yahweh awakened the spirit of Cyrus, the king of Persia, and he made a voice pass through all of his kingdom and also in writing, saying,
\v 23 “Thus says Cyrus, the king of Persia: ‘Yahweh, the God of the heavens, has given all of the kingdoms of the earth to me. And he, he has appointed me to build a house for him in Jerusalem, which is in Judah. Whoever among you {is} from all of his people, {may} Yahweh his God {be} with him, and let him go up.’” 